80
00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:12.400
and recovery and sobriety.

81
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.810
It's a major theme or topic that recurs on this show.

82
00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.880
And, I think a good place to kind of start with this

83
00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.270
is to share a little bit, you know,

84
00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.940
from my personal perspective.

85
00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:30.940
When I think back on my emotional state of being

86
00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:35.810
when I was at the nadir of my using

87
00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.740
and facing the prospect of getting sober,

88
00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:43.400
I just remember such a deep sense of shame

89
00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.400
and embarrassment and this sense of just being irreparably

90
00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:52.610
broken and being scared of everybody scared of everything,

91
00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.580
of the world, and this sense that, you know,

92
00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.250
really getting honest with myself about the fact

93
00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.910
that I was an addict really equated

94
00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.170
to being a broken human being

95
00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:09.120
and this pathology that it evokes, you know,

96
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.730
because my sense was that addicts were, you know,

97
00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:15.030
needle fiends and hopeless drunks,

98
00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:18.770
and that's kind of where the conversation begins and ends.

99
00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:22.330
But, as somebody who's been steeped in the recovery

100
00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.430
community and somebody who's been sober for a little while,

101
00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:29.533
I've become increasingly convinced over many years

102
00:05:29.533 --> 00:05:32.740
that that addiction is so misunderstood

103
00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.720
and so much more widely prevalent than I think we realize.

104
00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.300
And also that it exists on a spectrum that is so broad

105
00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:46.810
that it's not that much of a stretch to argue or contend

106
00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:50.610
that on some level we can all identify ourselves

107
00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.970
somewhere along that spectrum,

108
00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:55.910
whether it's a mild, unhealthy compulsion,

109
00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:59.620
all the way to some kind of full blown life implosion.

110
00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:04.210
And I'm interested in your sense of that because,

111
00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:08.007
I feel that your work and in particular this latest book,

112
00:06:08.007 --> 00:06:10.450
"Dopamine Nation" which we're gonna talk about,

113
00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:15.450
really, you know, is furthering that sensibility

114
00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:21.430
and providing that kind of perspective to a broad audience.

115
00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:24.740
I wanna first respond to your use

116
00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.450
of the word feeling broken.

117
00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:32.450
I think that is probably the single most powerful thing

118
00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:36.610
that has drawn me to people with addiction in recovery

119
00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:40.610
is that they've passed through that crucible

120
00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:45.110
of complete feelings of brokenness, humility,

121
00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:48.610
and they've come out the other side as these incredible

122
00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:51.680
humans with tremendous wisdom.

123
00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:54.240
And the book really is,

124
00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.099
you know, homage to people with addiction

125
00:06:58.099 --> 00:07:01.410
who have found recovery precisely

126
00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:05.920
because they have a profound humility

127
00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.830
having been through that experience.

128
00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.840
And although my addictions, which I talk about in the book

129
00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.840
are more minor than somebody with severe addiction-

130
00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:18.900
And somewhat comical.

131
00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.570
Somewhat comical, but, you know, for me,

132
00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:22.740
they were actually-
Real for you-

133
00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:23.573
Yeah, they were real-

134
00:07:23.573 --> 00:07:24.406
It's a subjective relationships.

135
00:07:24.406 --> 00:07:28.290
Right, it is, and because of the work that I do,

136
00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:32.210
I was at some point able to be aware of what was happening

137
00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.890
to me, that I don't think I would have been able to do

138
00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:38.620
had I not been in the line of work that I am.

139
00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:40.370
But I think most importantly,

140
00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:43.860
I've had other experiences in my life that have left me

141
00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:48.140
also feeling totally broken, a broken human, you know,

142
00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:53.030
full of shame, full of feeling like I wasn't good enough.

143
00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:56.630
And so I've really gravitated toward other people

144
00:07:56.630 --> 00:08:01.400
who have had that experience of brokenness

145
00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:06.040
and yet from it becomes stronger because of the profound

146
00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:10.070
humility that it engenders and a kind of surrender

147
00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:14.770
to the universe and a willingness to appreciate that.

148
00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:17.300
Like we have so little control.

149
00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:18.313
And all the other stuff,

150
00:08:18.313 --> 00:08:20.720
all the other good stuff that comes with that.

151
00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:23.813
Yeah. You share a quote near the beginning of the book

152
00:08:23.813 --> 00:08:27.897
that I love by this guy, Kent Dunnington, who says,

153
00:08:27.897 --> 00:08:31.110
"persons with severe addictions are among those contemporary

154
00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:34.510
profits that we ignore to our own demise for they show us

155
00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:35.590
who we truly are."

156
00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:37.610
Yes and that's why I'm meeting her for dinner.

157
00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:38.443
Oh, you are, cool.

158
00:08:38.443 --> 00:08:39.276
Who is Kent Dunnington?

159
00:08:39.276 --> 00:08:40.109
I don't know who he is.
Yeah,

160
00:08:40.109 --> 00:08:42.030
so he's a really interesting guy.

161
00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:44.210
He's a theologian and a philosopher.

162
00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:46.300
He works at Biola University.

163
00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.617
And I first came across a book he wrote called,

164
00:08:49.617 --> 00:08:51.830
"Addiction and Virtue."

165
00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:56.830
Which is this philosophical lens through to consider

166
00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.280
whether or not addiction really is a disease

167
00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.330
and whether or not people have a choice

168
00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:04.890
and what that choice looks like, and he uses Aristotle.

169
00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:08.300
And it's a fascinating look at that question

170
00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:11.660
because in the medical field now, I am really steeped

171
00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:13.930
in this idea that addiction is a disease.

172
00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:16.430
It's a chronic relapsing and remitting disease,

173
00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:19.810
there's, you know, verifiable brain chemistry

174
00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:22.320
changes that occurs, people become addicted,

175
00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.290
and yet, and yet, and I believe that that's true,

176
00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.810
and yet there is this element of choice, right?

177
00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:32.810
Somewhere along the way, people can choose to get help.

178
00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:35.430
Really that's the fundamental choice piece that's left.

179
00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:38.990
It's they can't necessarily choose to not drink or not use,

180
00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:43.990
but they can choose to say, I have, don't have the ability

181
00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:45.970
to choose or not choose to drink,

182
00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:49.260
but I do have the ability to hand it over to somebody else

183
00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:50.120
who might help me.

184
00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:51.480
Sure.
So anyway-

185
00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:55.030
But that element of choice is difficult.

186
00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:58.720
You know, often it's said that recovery is for people

187
00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:02.710
who want it, not those who need it and too often,

188
00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:07.710
the person who needs it is an is unable to make that choice.

189
00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:11.660
They're like the most unlikely person to make that choice.

190
00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:14.360
Yes, yes, I think that's right.

191
00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:15.193
That's right.

192
00:10:15.193 --> 00:10:19.270
I mean, and that's where this kind of mysterious thing

193
00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:23.230
happens where even people who really don't have the ability

194
00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:27.670
to make the choice can get into recovery, right?

195
00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:29.400
And we see that happen all the time.

196
00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:32.210
Right, and so, you know, in the context of this,

197
00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:33.720
we're gonna talk about neuroscience,

198
00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.120
we're gonna talk about psychology,

199
00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:36.930
we're gonna talk about environment, genetics,

200
00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:39.910
there's so many things that contribute to this.

201
00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:43.563
But in that context of choice,

202
00:10:45.609 --> 00:10:48.750
it never ceases to astound me that some people

203
00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:50.620
can just have an epiphany and say,

204
00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:52.590
this is a problem, and I'm gonna correct it,

205
00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:54.060
and they just do it.

206
00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.270
While others have to go to the gates of hell

207
00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:58.210
before they're ready to reckon with it.

208
00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:00.600
And too many just perish, you know,

209
00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:03.730
without ever having the courage to,

210
00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:05.800
you know, make that switch.

211
00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:07.540
Yes, that's right.

212
00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:12.270
It's so terrifying and sad in that regard, right?

213
00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:13.103
Yes.

214
00:11:13.103 --> 00:11:17.200
But my perspective is that in the typical case

215
00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:22.130
is the person who is kind of sliding down that scale

216
00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:24.950
with their addiction until they get to the point

217
00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:29.110
where the pain of perpetuating or continuing along that path

218
00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:31.930
exceeds the fear of letting go of it

219
00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:33.120
and trying something new.

220
00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:34.590
Yeah, that's right.

221
00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:37.723
That recognition that there's really no other option,

222
00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:40.750
but to embrace recovery.

223
00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:41.870
Mm-mmh.

224
00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:45.400
So why don't we define our terms here a little bit?

225
00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:47.473
Like, how do you define addiction?

226
00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:53.750
I define addiction as the continued compulsive use

227
00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:58.960
of a drug or behavior despite harm to self and or others.

228
00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:00.333
How do you define it?

229
00:12:00.333 --> 00:12:02.590
I mean, that's the standard definition.

230
00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:03.640
Yeah, right.

231
00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:07.140
And how does that, like,

232
00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:10.370
as I mentioned at the opening there's alcoholism,

233
00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:12.290
there's drug addiction,

234
00:12:12.290 --> 00:12:15.910
there are some qualitative differences between substance

235
00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:18.770
addiction and behavioral addictions.

236
00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:21.620
And now that we're in this world where everything

237
00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:24.320
has an addictive allure to it,

238
00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:28.570
do you qualify our relationship with our devices

239
00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:31.510
and online shopping and gambling in the same way,

240
00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:35.660
like with this idea of addiction being a spectrum,

241
00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:38.800
how does it differ in terms of like our relationship

242
00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:43.200
to Twitter versus our relationship to heroin?

243
00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.700
I don't really see all that much difference

244
00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:46.920
between those things.

245
00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:49.480
I think it's just a matter of degree.

246
00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:51.380
Obviously when we're talking about heroin,

247
00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:53.920
we're talking about the physiologic dependence

248
00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:56.880
and withdrawal as well, which I do think happens

249
00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:59.380
with behavioral addictions to things like Twitter.

250
00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:02.840
I believe we can have a true physiologic dependence

251
00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:05.200
and withdrawal to those behaviors,

252
00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:09.150
but not to the same degree as with something like an opioid,

253
00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:13.940
but in terms of the psychological aspect of the attachment,

254
00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:16.570
I think it's very, very similar.

255
00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:19.720
And I believe that because I've seen so many people

256
00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:23.720
come through my office who are addicted to things like

257
00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:26.580
gaming, gambling, pornography, shopping,

258
00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:29.407
and the natural history is exactly the same,

259
00:13:29.407 --> 00:13:32.130
and the manifestations are the same,

260
00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:34.340
the process of getting into recovery is the same.

261
00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:36.210
So to me, they're all one bucket.

262
00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:37.043
Mm-mmh.

263
00:13:37.043 --> 00:13:40.020
So is there a distinction between kind of an unhealthy

264
00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:44.160
recurring semi compulsive habit and what you would consider

265
00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:45.913
truly an addiction?

266
00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:51.930
Because both habits and addictive behaviors

267
00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:56.550
work through our dopamine reward pathway,

268
00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:59.650
I really think it's the same biology.

269
00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:01.530
It's just a matter of degree.

270
00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:02.363
Mm-mmh.

271
00:14:02.363 --> 00:14:03.850
And because I have seen people

272
00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:07.970
who can get severely addicted to behaviors like gambling,

273
00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:09.690
pornography, video games,

274
00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:11.610
to the point where they are suicidal

275
00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:16.610
so their life is threatened by that disease process

276
00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:17.763
or that behavior,

277
00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:22.350
I really think it's the same biochemistry.

278
00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:24.350
It's the same phenomenon in the brain.

279
00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:25.770
Yeah.

280
00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:29.890
It seems to me, we're in this weird time

281
00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:33.270
where on the one hand we use the word addiction

282
00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:36.610
very cavalierly, like, oh, I'm addicted to my TV show

283
00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:38.740
or chocolate, or what have you.

284
00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:43.740
And yet at the same time, we underappreciate the fact that,

285
00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:47.180
some of these things truly are addictions, right?

286
00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:49.590
Like these two things kind of cross each other

287
00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:51.220
in the night in a weird way.

288
00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:54.770
Which I think prevents us from really looking at them

289
00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:57.600
through the appropriate clinical lens.

290
00:14:57.600 --> 00:14:58.433
Yes. I think you're right.

291
00:14:58.433 --> 00:15:02.770
I think the way that we cavalierly refer to behaviors

292
00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:06.770
as addictions, oh, I am addicted to whatever my Netflix show

293
00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:12.810
does minimize the extent to which the brain biology

294
00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:20.150
is the same and also minimizes the impact on our lives.

295
00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:24.950
Because if we were to really scrutinize that behavior,

296
00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:28.230
I think people would begin to realize

297
00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:31.120
that it really truly does have an adverse impact

298
00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:32.510
on their lives,
Mm-hmm.

299
00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:37.400
and that, you know, we can't trivialize that.

300
00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:39.860
I mean, I've had, for example, you know,

301
00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:43.523
journalists writing for esteemed publications,

302
00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:47.347
call me to interview me about sex addiction and say to me,

303
00:15:47.347 --> 00:15:48.963
"well, that's not really an addiction, is it?

304
00:15:48.963 --> 00:15:52.990
I mean, it's really just about cultural morals around,

305
00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:56.210
you know, what's acceptable sexual behavior and what isn't."

306
00:15:56.210 --> 00:16:00.010
And I've had to correct that person say, no,

307
00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:01.320
you're you're wrong.

308
00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:03.570
This is really an addiction.

309
00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:05.670
It can devastate lives.

310
00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:09.100
People can lose their lives over these problems.

311
00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:13.120
And it's not just about, you know, oh, you know,

312
00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.840
polyamory should be socially accepted.

313
00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:17.650
That's not what we're talking about here.

314
00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:20.890
We're talking about a compulsive out of control phenomenon

315
00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:23.660
that has very serious consequences for people.

316
00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:26.490
Sure. If anybody questions that,

317
00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:28.237
I encourage you to read the first chapter of your book,

318
00:16:28.237 --> 00:16:32.490
because it's the most harrowing bone chilling story

319
00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:34.710
of a sex addict and the links

320
00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:37.960
that that guy goes to satisfy his need.

321
00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:38.793
Right.

322
00:16:38.793 --> 00:16:39.820
It's unbelievable.

323
00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:42.400
Yeah. And the fact that he's highly educated,

324
00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:45.090
that he's a good and kind person,

325
00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:47.150
that he has good social support,

326
00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:48.720
he has a great job.

327
00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:51.060
I mean, this is what I really want to drive home to people

328
00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:53.690
like, yeah, there are many risk factors for addiction,

329
00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:56.690
but you can have none of those risk factors in today's world

330
00:16:56.690 --> 00:16:58.080
and get really addicted.

331
00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:01.630
Mm-hmm. Do you feel like we're starting to understand

332
00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:05.670
that, in the sense that anybody can be an addict regardless

333
00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:09.880
of your social status or level of education, et cetera?

334
00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:10.863
I do think so.

335
00:17:10.863 --> 00:17:14.670
I mean, I do think we've moved beyond this moralizing

336
00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:18.440
around addiction as more and more people struggle

337
00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:21.870
with severe addiction and it crosses all demographics,

338
00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:25.410
all social classes, I think people, I mean, for example,

339
00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:28.330
in surveys of Americans asking whether or not they think

340
00:17:28.330 --> 00:17:30.930
addiction is a biologically based disease,

341
00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:34.030
the majority of Americans today will say, yes, it is.

342
00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:36.590
And that's different from 50 years ago.

343
00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:40.290
So I think we have shifted in that regard,

344
00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:45.277
but I think what's still new to people is the extent

345
00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:51.070
to which we've all become vulnerable to this problem.

346
00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:52.950
Yeah. That is a big difference.

347
00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:53.840
Yeah.
Right?

348
00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:58.420
And I do think, although we may recognize that that truth

349
00:17:58.420 --> 00:18:02.470
in that there is this biological component to it,

350
00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:05.840
we still are shocked when we hear the story of the upwardly

351
00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:08.450
mobile person who goes down some dark rabbit hole.

352
00:18:08.450 --> 00:18:11.170
Do you remember that, there was a New York Times article

353
00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:12.680
a couple of years ago about the guy,

354
00:18:12.680 --> 00:18:13.830
the lawyer in Silicon valley?

355
00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:14.663
Yes.

356
00:18:14.663 --> 00:18:16.770
That was like a huge profile of his second, you know,

357
00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:18.370
his other life.
Yes.

358
00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:21.140
And I just remember how, you know,

359
00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:23.160
people were so shocked by that story.

360
00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:24.347
Somebody who's been in recovery for a long time.

361
00:18:24.347 --> 00:18:27.219
I'm like, yeah, you know, I hear that story all the time.

362
00:18:27.219 --> 00:18:28.140
Right.

363
00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:29.890
I remember that being a big deal.

364
00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:34.500
Yeah. I think that there's still

365
00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:38.230
this kind of self-medication hypothesis thinking

366
00:18:38.230 --> 00:18:41.800
around addiction, which is only partially true.

367
00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:43.710
And the hypothesis goes like this,

368
00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:47.490
that addiction is the result of some other problem,

369
00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:52.490
either a willpower problem or a psychiatric illness problem,

370
00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:56.000
or, you know, a socio-cultural problem,

371
00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:58.440
but you can have none of those problems

372
00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:00.540
and still get addicted.

373
00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:05.460
And like I've spent a major part of my career reeducating

374
00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:08.850
in particular psychiatrists around this idea,

375
00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:11.620
because for so long, we've had this idea

376
00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:16.620
that addiction is a downstream consequence of some other

377
00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:19.970
problem, but addiction is its own problem.

378
00:19:19.970 --> 00:19:23.450
You can have a perfect life and get very, very addicted.

379
00:19:23.450 --> 00:19:24.283
Yeah. Yeah.

380
00:19:24.283 --> 00:19:26.300
That's a really important point.

381
00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:30.880
You know, I've had conversations with Gabor Maté

382
00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:33.350
and for him, it's all about childhood trauma,

383
00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:35.270
resolve that, you resolve the addiction.

384
00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:36.840
I had Johann Hari here.

385
00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:38.900
For him it's all about loss connections,

386
00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:41.240
your connectivity to your friends

387
00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:42.650
and your family and your community.

388
00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:46.690
And I think those are important pieces in this puzzle,

389
00:19:46.690 --> 00:19:51.690
but I'm reluctant to be reductionist about the role

390
00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:56.920
that those play in the broader context of addiction.

391
00:19:56.920 --> 00:20:00.140
Thank you so much for invoking those examples,

392
00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:03.770
because that shows me that you perfectly understand

393
00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:07.500
what I'm trying to communicate here, because yes, it's true

394
00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:09.830
that childhood trauma increases your risk

395
00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:11.270
for developing addiction.

396
00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:15.150
Yes, it's true that addiction leads to isolation

397
00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:17.550
and that the antidote to addiction

398
00:20:17.550 --> 00:20:19.920
is social connection in part.

399
00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:23.060
But what is so important to understand

400
00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:26.970
is you can have the perfect childhood, the perfect parents,

401
00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:29.850
the greatest social network, the best spouse,

402
00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:34.210
wonderful kids, and you can get really, really addicted.

403
00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:37.470
And that is so important for people to understand,

404
00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:39.410
and also healthcare providers,

405
00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:41.980
because everybody's sort of looking for the reason

406
00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:43.007
behind the addiction,

407
00:20:43.007 --> 00:20:45.980
but there doesn't have to be a reason behind addiction.

408
00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:48.380
Addiction just can be on its own.

409
00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:51.000
Yeah, I think that's a crucial point.

410
00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.550
I mean, I get asked very frequently,

411
00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:55.370
like, what do you think caused this?

412
00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:57.240
Like, what is the source of your addiction?

413
00:20:57.240 --> 00:21:01.410
And, you know, I'm heavily indoctrinated in 12 step

414
00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:03.970
and I'm sure I have some biases around that.

415
00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:07.230
But one of the things that you learn is that it's fine

416
00:21:07.230 --> 00:21:09.760
if you want a psychoanalysis and analyze

417
00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:13.350
that aspect of your origin story, but ultimately,

418
00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:15.990
it doesn't avail you with the tools for how to live today

419
00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:19.600
and how to move productively forward.

420
00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:22.570
And, you know, there is an argument to be made

421
00:21:22.570 --> 00:21:24.880
that it's sort of a fool's errand

422
00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:26.730
to spend too much time on that.

423
00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:29.214
Yes. Insight can be the booby prize.

424
00:21:29.214 --> 00:21:30.120
Yeah.

425
00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:34.090
Yeah, no, this is a really very, very important concept.

426
00:21:34.090 --> 00:21:36.970
And the other reason it's important is because I think

427
00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:41.140
we're natural storytellers and we want to rationalize

428
00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:42.680
irrational behavior.

429
00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:47.320
And so, the first thing that we try to do when we are doing

430
00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:50.370
something that's irrational and self and other destructive,

431
00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:53.170
is to tell ourselves a story or have someone else

432
00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:56.750
tell us a story about why we would do that crazy thing.

433
00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:57.583
But-

434
00:21:57.583 --> 00:21:58.760
And if we can solve that equation,

435
00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:00.159
then suddenly every everything makes sense-

436
00:22:00.159 --> 00:22:00.992
Right.

437
00:22:00.992 --> 00:22:02.220
And you can figure this out.

438
00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:04.121
But it doesn't work out that way.

439
00:22:04.121 --> 00:22:07.930
No, I mean, I have had over 20 plus years,

440
00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:10.637
countless patients come into my office and say,

441
00:22:10.637 --> 00:22:13.330
"Dr. Lembke, the reason that I'm addicted to alcohol

442
00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:16.650
or I'm addicted to cannabis, or I'm compulsive gambler

443
00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:18.290
is because I'm depressed and anxious.

444
00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:19.543
And if you would just fix that,

445
00:22:19.543 --> 00:22:21.940
then I wouldn't have that other problem."

446
00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:24.440
And what I have to do is say to them, you know what?

447
00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:27.860
I wish that were true, but here's the truth.

448
00:22:27.860 --> 00:22:31.020
Number one, even if I could magically wave my wand

449
00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:33.050
and make your depression and anxiety go away,

450
00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:35.040
once you're addicted, you're addicted,

451
00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:36.920
and if we don't focus on that problem,

452
00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:39.240
that's not gonna get better.

453
00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:41.120
The other thing is that, you know,

454
00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:44.820
that relationship between psychological symptoms

455
00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:48.670
and addiction, it's complicated and it's not necessarily

456
00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:50.470
that the depression comes first

457
00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:51.800
and then the addiction comes.

458
00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:54.010
Addiction can lead to depression and anxiety,

459
00:22:54.010 --> 00:22:57.150
which is why my first intervention so often

460
00:22:57.150 --> 00:22:59.010
is to have people abstain.

461
00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:00.677
But I'll never forget a patient of mine who said,

462
00:23:00.677 --> 00:23:04.460
"You know, Dr. Lembke, I realized I was an alcoholic

463
00:23:04.460 --> 00:23:06.630
when I got started on an antidepressant,

464
00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:09.030
wasn't depressed anymore, but kept drinking."

465
00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:11.350
That was his aha moment because he was like,

466
00:23:11.350 --> 00:23:13.700
oh, I thought I was drinking, 'cause I was depressed.

467
00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:16.290
But when I stopped being depressed, I was still drinking.

468
00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:18.200
Yeah. That's kind of the genius behind

469
00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:20.350
your dual-diagnosis clinic, right?

470
00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:24.080
It's almost a Trojan horse way of just treating addiction

471
00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:26.830
because the way you get them in the door

472
00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:29.160
in a non-threatening way is under the rubric

473
00:23:29.160 --> 00:23:32.380
of treating their anxiety or depression, et cetera.

474
00:23:32.380 --> 00:23:33.450
Right, yes.
Yeah.

475
00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:36.700
Although it's not without effort because people

476
00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:38.860
are resistant to that idea, right?

477
00:23:38.860 --> 00:23:42.520
They come in and they want help with depression and anxiety,

478
00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:45.530
they see the addiction as a secondary problem,

479
00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:49.820
and it takes all my powers of persuasion

480
00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:51.303
to get them on board.

481
00:23:51.303 --> 00:23:52.980
(both laughing)

482
00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:54.743
None too happy to be told that can't deal

483
00:23:54.743 --> 00:23:58.960
with your condition X until we deal with this substance

484
00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:00.877
or behavioral addiction that you have.

485
00:24:00.877 --> 00:24:04.550
And they storm out, leave a one star yelp review

486
00:24:04.550 --> 00:24:05.383
or something.

487
00:24:05.383 --> 00:24:08.415
Exactly. You you've got it exactly, you understand.

488
00:24:08.415 --> 00:24:09.750
(both laughing)

489
00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:11.500
Yeah. If people don't wanna hear that, you know,

490
00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:13.760
it's interesting that, that piece, I mean,

491
00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:17.660
as a psychiatrist though, you must have honed tools

492
00:24:17.660 --> 00:24:19.260
for how to communicate with people

493
00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:21.040
to kind of crack that core.

494
00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:26.040
Yes. And the key piece is to be empathic

495
00:24:26.920 --> 00:24:28.540
at the same time that you're telling people

496
00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:29.890
what they don't wanna hear.

497
00:24:30.760 --> 00:24:31.714
You know what I mean?
That's tough.

498
00:24:31.714 --> 00:24:32.730
That's a tight rope on.

499
00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:33.760
It is. It is.

500
00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:35.410
But I feel like I've gotten pretty good at it.

501
00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:38.040
Having teenagers also helps, you know,

502
00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:41.487
to sort of you breathe and you stay calm and you say,

503
00:24:41.487 --> 00:24:43.810
you know, I understand what you're saying, right?

504
00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:46.510
So the typical sort of validating first,

505
00:24:46.510 --> 00:24:50.000
but then you give them kind of the real deal.

506
00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:51.920
You know, what the science shows,

507
00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:54.390
what my clinical experience shows,

508
00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:57.570
what I'm asking them to experiment with,

509
00:24:57.570 --> 00:24:59.400
so that they can gather their own data and see

510
00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:01.120
if what I'm saying is right.

511
00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:04.550
But on the data piece, I mean, this doesn't operate,

512
00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:06.270
you know, in a logical framework,

513
00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:07.720
it's an emotional framework

514
00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:09.620
and timing is so important, right?

515
00:25:09.620 --> 00:25:13.090
Like I'm happy to talk sobriety with anyone who's suffering,

516
00:25:13.090 --> 00:25:16.870
but I've learned to detach or from any expectations

517
00:25:16.870 --> 00:25:18.750
of what they will or won't do.

518
00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:20.950
Like people get sober when they're ready to.

519
00:25:20.950 --> 00:25:22.940
Yeah. And one of the main things

520
00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:27.223
I have to teach my trainees, my fellows is that,

521
00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:31.030
an essential part of the work that we do is that,

522
00:25:31.030 --> 00:25:34.470
we have to actually really care about our patients,

523
00:25:34.470 --> 00:25:37.410
but there is a point at which you can care too much.

524
00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:40.220
And when we're trying harder than the patient is,

525
00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:42.230
or wanting it more than the patient wants it,

526
00:25:42.230 --> 00:25:43.750
we're not actually helping them.

527
00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:45.460
Then you gotta go to the Al-Anon meetings-

528
00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:46.401
That's right.

529
00:25:46.401 --> 00:25:47.330
(both laughing)

530
00:25:47.330 --> 00:25:49.570
Well, we have to conduct our own little Al-Anon session,

531
00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:50.403
you know?
Right.

532
00:25:50.403 --> 00:25:51.236
Right there at work.

533
00:25:51.236 --> 00:25:53.020
'Cause I work with a bunch of young folks,

534
00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:54.670
which I can say is one of the most exciting things

535
00:25:54.670 --> 00:25:56.630
that's happened in the last 10 years.

536
00:25:56.630 --> 00:25:59.600
You know, 10 years ago, I couldn't find a medical student

537
00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:01.410
under a rock who wanted to learn about addiction.

538
00:26:01.410 --> 00:26:04.060
Now they're beating down my door, which is really awesome.

539
00:26:04.060 --> 00:26:07.450
Yeah. Well, let's talk about the neurochemistry

540
00:26:07.450 --> 00:26:08.970
of addiction.

541
00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:11.600
Walk me through what's happening in our brains

542
00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:13.880
and the role that dopamine plays in all of this.

543
00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:17.500
Yeah. So dopamine is a neurotransmitter,

544
00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:20.620
which means that it is the molecule that allows

545
00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:23.620
the electrical signal from the pre synaptic neuron

546
00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:25.550
to be communicated to the post synaptic neuron,

547
00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:27.610
because there's a little gap called the synapse

548
00:26:27.610 --> 00:26:28.910
between those two neurons.

549
00:26:29.889 --> 00:26:32.020
So neuro-transmitters allow fine tuning

550
00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:33.470
of those electrical signals.

551
00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:36.320
And dopamine is the most important neurotransmitter

552
00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:39.290
involved in motivation and reward.

553
00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:41.590
And the fundamental difference between things

554
00:26:41.590 --> 00:26:44.390
that are addictive and those that aren't is that things

555
00:26:44.390 --> 00:26:47.860
that are addictive release a lot more dopamine.

556
00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:50.850
So we have dopamine firing in our brain that occurs

557
00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:53.750
at a tonic baseline, and when we do something

558
00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:55.660
that's rewarding or pleasurable,

559
00:26:55.660 --> 00:26:59.540
we get a little rise in dopamine levels or a spike.

560
00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:02.700
So for example, chocolate increases dopamine levels

561
00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:05.370
about 50% above baseline.

562
00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:07.110
Sex is about 100%.

563
00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:09.200
Nicotine is about 150%.

564
00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:13.240
And things like methamphetamines are 1000%

565
00:27:13.240 --> 00:27:16.780
partially because of their specific mechanism.

566
00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:20.120
But the fundamental way that I explain to patients

567
00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:22.830
and medical students, and now in my book

568
00:27:22.830 --> 00:27:25.540
about the neuroscience of addiction so that they can really

569
00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:28.110
understand what's happening in the brain is,

570
00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:32.760
I say that really, you have to imagine that in your brain,

571
00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:35.690
there's a balance like a teeter-totter and a playground.

572
00:27:35.690 --> 00:27:38.440
When we experience pleasure the balance tips one way

573
00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:41.263
when we experience pain, it tips the other.

574
00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:45.510
But one of the fundamental rules governing that balance

575
00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:48.150
is that it wants to remain level.

576
00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:50.830
So with any deviation from neutrality,

577
00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:53.860
the brain will work very hard to restore a level of balance

578
00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:56.223
or what's called homeostasis.

579
00:27:57.180 --> 00:27:59.890
So for example, if I do something pleasurable,

580
00:27:59.890 --> 00:28:01.730
like eat a piece of chocolate,

581
00:28:01.730 --> 00:28:03.280
I get a little tip to the side of pleasure,

582
00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:04.520
a little release of dopamine,

583
00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:06.600
but no sooner that has that happened,

584
00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:10.380
than my brain adapts to that phenomenon by down-regulating

585
00:28:10.380 --> 00:28:12.380
my own dopamine receptors,

586
00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:14.720
down-regulating my own dopamine transmission.

587
00:28:14.720 --> 00:28:16.620
And I imagine that as these little gremlins

588
00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:18.910
hopping on the pain side of the balance

589
00:28:18.910 --> 00:28:20.131
to bring it level again.

590
00:28:20.131 --> 00:28:20.964
Mm-mmh.

591
00:28:20.964 --> 00:28:21.960
But the thing about the gremlins is,

592
00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:23.050
they like it on the balance.

593
00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:26.000
So they stay on until the balance is tipped an equal

594
00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:28.380
and opposite amount to the side of pain.

595
00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:30.980
And that's called the opponent process reaction,

596
00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:34.650
the hangover, the come down, the after effect.

597
00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:36.700
Now, and in my case, that's that moment of wanting

598
00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:38.050
another piece of chocolate.

599
00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:41.230
If I wait long enough the gremlin hops off

600
00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:42.780
and balance is restored.

601
00:28:42.780 --> 00:28:44.980
But if I continue to consume chocolate

602
00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:47.910
in ever larger amounts to overcome the tolerance

603
00:28:47.910 --> 00:28:50.090
or the number of gremlins on the pain side,

604
00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:52.230
then I end up with enough gremlins on the pain side

605
00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:54.510
of my balance to fill this whole room.

606
00:28:54.510 --> 00:28:57.670
And I'm essentially in a dopamine deficit state

607
00:28:57.670 --> 00:29:00.550
with a balanced tilted to the side of pain.

608
00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:03.370
Now I have to keep using not to feel good,

609
00:29:03.370 --> 00:29:05.670
but just to feel normal.

610
00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:09.170
And when I stop using my balance tips hard to the side

611
00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:12.700
of pain, I'm irritable, I'm depressed, I'm anxious,

612
00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:13.630
I can't sleep.

613
00:29:13.630 --> 00:29:15.790
Those are the universal symptoms of withdrawal

614
00:29:15.790 --> 00:29:17.780
from any addictive substance.

615
00:29:17.780 --> 00:29:20.450
And that can last a long time.

616
00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:23.650
Yeah. I think it brings up an important kind

617
00:29:23.650 --> 00:29:28.650
of broader point about culture in general and this idea

618
00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:35.810
that we live in a situation in which we are...

619
00:29:37.090 --> 00:29:40.580
There's this asymmetry in terms of how we approach our lives

620
00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:42.220
with respect to pleasure and pain.

621
00:29:42.220 --> 00:29:46.070
And we organize our lives completely around the pursuit

622
00:29:46.070 --> 00:29:48.710
of pleasure and the avoidance of pain.

623
00:29:48.710 --> 00:29:51.430
But as you kind of astutely put in the book,

624
00:29:51.430 --> 00:29:55.220
the more that we pursue that, ultimately the more pain

625
00:29:55.220 --> 00:29:59.610
that we reap, because these systems require a level

626
00:29:59.610 --> 00:30:02.410
of homeostasis that we're constantly trying to avoid.

627
00:30:02.410 --> 00:30:07.293
So, we're just delaying the inevitable onslaught of pain

628
00:30:07.293 --> 00:30:10.270
that, you know, we so fear.

629
00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:11.660
Yes, absolutely.

630
00:30:11.660 --> 00:30:15.980
We live in a world in which we are saturated with dopamine

631
00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:19.520
and we live in a culture which encourages us to pursue it.

632
00:30:19.520 --> 00:30:22.900
But the ultimate end result of pursuing dopamine

633
00:30:22.900 --> 00:30:25.360
is to feel worse than when you started.

634
00:30:25.360 --> 00:30:27.893
And this is really the central message.

635
00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:33.870
People are more depressed, more anxious, more suicidal,

636
00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:36.310
and more addicted than they were 30 years ago.

637
00:30:36.310 --> 00:30:39.280
And I contend that one of the main reasons

638
00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:43.490
is because of this relentless pursuit of pleasure

639
00:30:43.490 --> 00:30:47.130
that essentially adjusts the dopamine levels,

640
00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:51.060
changes the hedonic or seizure or pleasure set point

641
00:30:51.060 --> 00:30:54.490
to make people anhedonic meaning without joy.

642
00:30:54.490 --> 00:30:57.280
Yeah. And you can kind of calibrate this in lockstep

643
00:30:57.280 --> 00:31:02.090
with how we've progressed from a world of scarcity

644
00:31:02.090 --> 00:31:04.790
into a world of over abundance, right?

645
00:31:04.790 --> 00:31:08.870
And so, these addictive behaviors and substances

646
00:31:08.870 --> 00:31:10.390
are ubiquitous.

647
00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:14.090
It used to just be, hard drugs and alcohol

648
00:31:14.090 --> 00:31:15.930
and cigarettes, et cetera.

649
00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:19.340
But now it's literally everything from, you know,

650
00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:21.800
the billboards and the advertisements

651
00:31:21.800 --> 00:31:25.040
and the marketing messages crafted by Madison Avenue

652
00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:29.170
to of course, the devices that we all carry around with us

653
00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:32.740
that are specifically designed to lure us into this,

654
00:31:32.740 --> 00:31:37.210
you know, dopamine induced state and the food system,

655
00:31:37.210 --> 00:31:40.070
that's specifically designing foods with the right

656
00:31:40.070 --> 00:31:43.260
proportion of salt, sugar, and fat to make it impossible

657
00:31:43.260 --> 00:31:45.750
to just eat one potato chip.

658
00:31:45.750 --> 00:31:46.720
Yes, exactly.

659
00:31:46.720 --> 00:31:50.070
I mean, addiction can happen now in every realm of life,

660
00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:52.400
it's almost impossible to escape it.

661
00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:55.850
And the three factors that make anything more addictive

662
00:31:55.850 --> 00:31:59.100
are quantity, potency and novelty.

663
00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:03.800
And what our capitalist technologically innovative world

664
00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:08.390
has created is, an infinite quantity, incredible potency,

665
00:32:08.390 --> 00:32:10.540
and endless novelty and variety.

666
00:32:10.540 --> 00:32:12.900
Yeah. And ubiquitous availability.

667
00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:13.901
Yes, right.
Right?

668
00:32:13.901 --> 00:32:14.990
It's unavoidable.

669
00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:15.890
Yes, right.
Now.

670
00:32:15.890 --> 00:32:17.870
And now it's 24/7 and it's digital

671
00:32:17.870 --> 00:32:19.750
and it's right to your doorstep, exactly.

672
00:32:19.750 --> 00:32:21.330
Right. Everything's delivered as well.

673
00:32:21.330 --> 00:32:22.163
Yes, right.

674
00:32:22.163 --> 00:32:23.011
Including all the substances.

675
00:32:23.011 --> 00:32:24.550
Right.
(both laughing)

676
00:32:24.550 --> 00:32:25.540
Which we're going to get into.

677
00:32:25.540 --> 00:32:30.473
But, one curious thing for me back to the neurochemistry is,

678
00:32:31.805 --> 00:32:34.400
you know, why someone becomes, if this, you know,

679
00:32:34.400 --> 00:32:36.390
dopamine neurotransmitter pathway

680
00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:39.670
that you're speaking about, seems to be, you know,

681
00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:42.380
a general quality of all humans,

682
00:32:42.380 --> 00:32:44.940
why do some people get addicted and why some people don't?

683
00:32:44.940 --> 00:32:46.840
And, you know, for example, like,

684
00:32:46.840 --> 00:32:49.220
alcohol is my drug of choice,

685
00:32:49.220 --> 00:32:52.800
gambling, which debilitates a lot of people

686
00:32:52.800 --> 00:32:55.100
carries no charge for me at all.

687
00:32:55.100 --> 00:32:57.370
Like I just couldn't be less interested in it.

688
00:32:57.370 --> 00:32:59.270
Like, how does that work?

689
00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:02.800
Is that where genetics and nurturing and all kinds

690
00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:04.600
of other things come into play?

691
00:33:04.600 --> 00:33:07.200
Well, first let me speak to the concept of drug of choice,

692
00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:09.080
'cause it's a fascinating one and there's actually

693
00:33:09.080 --> 00:33:11.220
very little science around it,

694
00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:14.320
but we know phenomenologically that it's true.

695
00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:17.330
What makes one person's balance tip to the side of pleasure,

696
00:33:17.330 --> 00:33:19.890
doesn't necessarily make another person's balance tip

697
00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:21.110
to the side of pleasure.

698
00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:22.580
So, for me personally,

699
00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:25.070
I thought that I was immune to the problem of addiction

700
00:33:25.070 --> 00:33:28.017
because the substances that are readily available

701
00:33:28.017 --> 00:33:30.250
and that most people get addicted to,

702
00:33:30.250 --> 00:33:32.500
do absolutely nothing for me.

703
00:33:32.500 --> 00:33:33.870
But look, what happened to me.

704
00:33:33.870 --> 00:33:38.870
I did find a substance that turned out to be incredibly

705
00:33:39.700 --> 00:33:42.697
reinforcing for me, essentially, romance novels.

706
00:33:42.697 --> 00:33:44.287
Right.
Right. I know you laugh,

707
00:33:44.287 --> 00:33:46.443
(Rich laughing)
you laugh, but I mean, it was-

708
00:33:46.443 --> 00:33:49.820
Did you really read the entire Twilight series four times?

709
00:33:49.820 --> 00:33:51.900
I did, that was my gateway drug.

710
00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:52.733
Are you kidding?

711
00:33:52.733 --> 00:33:53.566
I really did.

712
00:33:53.566 --> 00:33:54.730
And then I moved on from there.

713
00:33:54.730 --> 00:33:57.100
'Cause I developed tolerance after the fourth time.

714
00:33:57.100 --> 00:33:58.210
It was no longer doing it for you.

715
00:33:58.210 --> 00:34:00.260
It wasn't, it wasn't good enough.

716
00:34:00.260 --> 00:34:02.093
And it was really mysterious 'cause I was like, you know,

717
00:34:02.093 --> 00:34:05.220
this is like a teenage romance novel about vampires

718
00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:06.670
and I was in my 40's.

719
00:34:06.670 --> 00:34:09.610
So I mean that in itself is slightly embarrassing,

720
00:34:09.610 --> 00:34:14.127
but it just led to this down this whole weird road for me.

721
00:34:14.127 --> 00:34:15.580
"Anna 50 Shades of Gray".

722
00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:16.780
Right, right.

723
00:34:16.780 --> 00:34:17.613
That'll do the trick.

724
00:34:17.613 --> 00:34:19.500
That was my bottom right there.

725
00:34:19.500 --> 00:34:20.633
No pun intended.

726
00:34:23.317 --> 00:34:26.340
So, I guess my point, you know,

727
00:34:26.340 --> 00:34:29.963
to answer your question is that,

728
00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:36.410
like, I think we're all gonna get addicted to something

729
00:34:36.410 --> 00:34:39.880
because now that special key that works

730
00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:41.310
for each of our individual locks,

731
00:34:41.310 --> 00:34:43.410
it's out there somewhere and the worldwide web

732
00:34:43.410 --> 00:34:44.840
will allow us to find it.

733
00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:48.860
Having said that, it is true that people bring different

734
00:34:48.860 --> 00:34:52.710
degrees of vulnerability to the process of addiction.

735
00:34:52.710 --> 00:34:55.740
We do know that about 50-60% of the risk

736
00:34:55.740 --> 00:34:57.860
of becoming addicted is genetic,

737
00:34:57.860 --> 00:34:59.500
that's based on family studies,

738
00:34:59.500 --> 00:35:01.400
showing that if you have a biological parent

739
00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:03.620
or grandparent addicted to alcohol,

740
00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:06.540
you are at increased risk of becoming an alcoholic yourself,

741
00:35:06.540 --> 00:35:09.290
even if you're raised outside of the alcoholic home,

742
00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:11.030
in a non using home.

743
00:35:11.030 --> 00:35:12.830
So, so that's powerful genetics.

744
00:35:12.830 --> 00:35:14.780
It's Polytechnic, it's complex,

745
00:35:14.780 --> 00:35:16.640
we don't fully understand it,

746
00:35:16.640 --> 00:35:19.760
it's thought to be related to things like impulse control,

747
00:35:19.760 --> 00:35:24.470
ability to delay gratification, emotional dysregulation.

748
00:35:24.470 --> 00:35:26.630
But, you know, we don't really know what it is.

749
00:35:26.630 --> 00:35:28.040
Other risk factors include,

750
00:35:28.040 --> 00:35:29.770
co-occurring psychiatric disorders.

751
00:35:29.770 --> 00:35:32.670
People with psychiatric disorders are more likely to develop

752
00:35:32.670 --> 00:35:35.120
an addiction, and also how you were raised.

753
00:35:35.120 --> 00:35:37.450
If you had a traumatic experience, as we've about,

754
00:35:37.450 --> 00:35:39.540
that puts you at risk.

755
00:35:39.540 --> 00:35:43.000
If you have parents who have explicitly or implicitly

756
00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:45.990
condoned substance use, either for recreation

757
00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:48.920
or as a coping strategy, that puts you at risk.

758
00:35:48.920 --> 00:35:52.360
Things like poverty, unemployment, that puts you at risk.

759
00:35:52.360 --> 00:35:54.610
So there are lots and lots of risk factors,

760
00:35:54.610 --> 00:35:57.140
but I think that the major risk factor

761
00:35:57.140 --> 00:36:00.530
in the modern world and one which is generally ignored,

762
00:36:00.530 --> 00:36:02.210
is simple access.

763
00:36:02.210 --> 00:36:06.300
If you have access to a drug, you are more likely to try it

764
00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:08.020
and more likely to get addicted to it.

765
00:36:08.020 --> 00:36:09.420
And now, as we've talked about,

766
00:36:09.420 --> 00:36:12.630
we live in a world of virtually infinite access.

767
00:36:12.630 --> 00:36:14.540
Yeah. You say there's a quote in the book,

768
00:36:14.540 --> 00:36:16.730
something along the lines of, whatever....

769
00:36:16.730 --> 00:36:18.230
There is something that will addict you

770
00:36:18.230 --> 00:36:19.930
and it's coming to a website near you soon.

771
00:36:19.930 --> 00:36:21.790
Correct. Right.
(both laughing)

772
00:36:21.790 --> 00:36:23.890
Yeah. And it's never been more true,

773
00:36:23.890 --> 00:36:27.450
but that idea that, there is a drug out there

774
00:36:27.450 --> 00:36:30.680
that will be the key to your specific lock,

775
00:36:30.680 --> 00:36:32.720
I think is super interesting.

776
00:36:32.720 --> 00:36:34.920
And you've used the example in the book

777
00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:38.020
of this Stanford student who had all manner of problems

778
00:36:38.020 --> 00:36:42.010
and kept screwing his life up and tried all different kinds

779
00:36:42.010 --> 00:36:43.892
of drugs and would come back to Stanford

780
00:36:43.892 --> 00:36:46.970
and just go down some crazy drug rabbit hole and drop out.

