00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:30.460
And then of course, I absolutely agree with you.

1036
00:48:30.460 --> 00:48:32.190
I mean, when people say they've tried it in the past

1037
00:48:32.190 --> 00:48:34.810
and didn't work, I'm always like, well, try it again.

1038
00:48:34.810 --> 00:48:37.740
You know, this might be the time that it really takes.

1039
00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:40.680
And for my most severely addicted patients,

1040
00:48:40.680 --> 00:48:45.680
it's almost universally, the best option.

1041
00:48:46.010 --> 00:48:49.530
And the data support that for the more severely addicted,

1042
00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:53.260
12 steps may actually work better than anything else.

1043
00:48:53.260 --> 00:48:55.060
People get tripped up on the God part.

1044
00:48:55.060 --> 00:48:56.486
Yes they do.

1045
00:48:56.486 --> 00:48:57.319
Yeah.

1046
00:48:57.319 --> 00:48:59.830
So, I'm interested in as a neuroscientist,

1047
00:48:59.830 --> 00:49:04.760
as a psychiatrist, how do you conceptualize the role

1048
00:49:04.760 --> 00:49:06.410
of spirituality in all of this?

1049
00:49:06.410 --> 00:49:11.410
Because it is such a core precept tenant of 12 step.

1050
00:49:11.890 --> 00:49:14.230
And I think that gets back to how we started,

1051
00:49:14.230 --> 00:49:17.363
where you talked about just feeling totally broken.

1052
00:49:18.550 --> 00:49:22.270
To me that is the fundamental spiritual pivot.

1053
00:49:22.270 --> 00:49:25.130
When we acknowledge our brokenness,

1054
00:49:25.130 --> 00:49:28.360
that is when we can give it over to something outside

1055
00:49:28.360 --> 00:49:31.960
of ourselves, and that can take many different forms.

1056
00:49:31.960 --> 00:49:35.450
But the key piece there is acknowledging

1057
00:49:35.450 --> 00:49:37.850
that we are not in control

1058
00:49:38.730 --> 00:49:43.730
and that when we ask the universe such as it were, you know,

1059
00:49:45.400 --> 00:49:47.530
to guide us or help us,

1060
00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:51.830
that simple reorientation totally changes,

1061
00:49:51.830 --> 00:49:55.960
like decision-making, it changes so many things

1062
00:49:55.960 --> 00:49:59.650
about how we proceed in our lives, the sort of, you know,

1063
00:49:59.650 --> 00:50:04.520
cognitive math of decision-making, or what to do next.

1064
00:50:04.520 --> 00:50:09.520
And, and I know I've experienced that, for other reasons,

1065
00:50:10.040 --> 00:50:11.920
not related to addiction.

1066
00:50:11.920 --> 00:50:14.380
And I know that's the fundamental pivot that my patients

1067
00:50:14.380 --> 00:50:17.120
experience that kind of feeling utterly broken,

1068
00:50:17.120 --> 00:50:19.970
and then looking outside themselves,

1069
00:50:19.970 --> 00:50:21.950
something larger than themselves

1070
00:50:23.100 --> 00:50:25.750
in order to pick those pieces up again and move on.

1071
00:50:25.750 --> 00:50:28.470
Yeah. That idea of surrender

1072
00:50:28.470 --> 00:50:31.742
and the kind of humility that inculcates with that,

1073
00:50:31.742 --> 00:50:35.800
is a tall mountain to climb for a lot of people,

1074
00:50:35.800 --> 00:50:37.390
and it was, it was difficult for me,

1075
00:50:37.390 --> 00:50:39.800
but that's really where things kind of open up

1076
00:50:39.800 --> 00:50:42.350
and you are able to reframe your relationship

1077
00:50:42.350 --> 00:50:43.400
with how you're living.

1078
00:50:43.400 --> 00:50:44.463
Yes. Right.

1079
00:50:44.463 --> 00:50:48.100
It's a total game changer really,

1080
00:50:48.100 --> 00:50:50.313
when you make that pivot.

1081
00:50:51.490 --> 00:50:55.390
And it's amazing the good things that come from doing that.

1082
00:50:55.390 --> 00:50:57.020
Yeah.

1083
00:50:57.020 --> 00:51:00.320
Let's talk about withdrawal a little bit,

1084
00:51:00.320 --> 00:51:03.620
back to the biochemistry of everything.

1085
00:51:03.620 --> 00:51:06.650
Obviously, you know, every substance has a different

1086
00:51:06.650 --> 00:51:10.660
half-life and the withdrawal from whatever you're doing

1087
00:51:10.660 --> 00:51:13.930
is gonna be different, but what's going on in your brain,

1088
00:51:13.930 --> 00:51:17.470
you talked about the kind of seesaw and the way

1089
00:51:17.470 --> 00:51:20.640
that dopamine operates when somebody is withdrawing

1090
00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:24.040
from a substance and they're kind of experiencing

1091
00:51:24.040 --> 00:51:26.170
the pain that comes with that.

1092
00:51:26.170 --> 00:51:30.790
What is going on and what does it take to kind of get past

1093
00:51:30.790 --> 00:51:32.093
that to the other side?

1094
00:51:33.490 --> 00:51:36.160
So there's a distinction between acute withdrawal

1095
00:51:36.160 --> 00:51:38.020
and protracted withdrawal.

1096
00:51:38.020 --> 00:51:42.120
Acute withdrawal is essentially where the body manifests

1097
00:51:42.120 --> 00:51:44.970
the opposite of whatever the drug does.

1098
00:51:44.970 --> 00:51:47.060
So if you have been using a stimulant,

1099
00:51:47.060 --> 00:51:49.670
then an acute withdrawal you will be sedated.

1100
00:51:49.670 --> 00:51:52.590
If you've been using a sedative, then an acute withdrawal

1101
00:51:52.590 --> 00:51:55.650
you will have physiologic restlessness.

1102
00:51:55.650 --> 00:51:58.020
And that can last anywhere between a few days

1103
00:51:58.020 --> 00:52:01.150
to a few weeks, depending upon the substance

1104
00:52:01.150 --> 00:52:03.540
and its half-life as you point out.

1105
00:52:03.540 --> 00:52:06.620
But once you get through the acute physiologic withdrawal,

1106
00:52:06.620 --> 00:52:09.650
I think what's underappreciated generally

1107
00:52:09.650 --> 00:52:13.070
is that there can be this sustained protracted withdrawal

1108
00:52:13.070 --> 00:52:16.730
that can go on for months, and in some cases, even years,

1109
00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:20.340
which is primarily psychological symptoms,

1110
00:52:20.340 --> 00:52:24.450
again, irritability, anxiety, depression, and insomnia,

1111
00:52:24.450 --> 00:52:25.830
as well as craving.

1112
00:52:25.830 --> 00:52:28.920
So this is like ruminative obsessive thinking

1113
00:52:28.920 --> 00:52:33.060
about wanting to use, and that can even be accompanied

1114
00:52:33.060 --> 00:52:38.060
by sudden physiologic feelings, sweating, stomach cramps.

1115
00:52:39.210 --> 00:52:44.210
But that's the piece that in my mind I visualize

1116
00:52:44.640 --> 00:52:46.100
the pleasure pain balance,

1117
00:52:46.100 --> 00:52:47.720
chronically weighted to the side of pain

1118
00:52:47.720 --> 00:52:49.910
because those neuro adaptation gremlins

1119
00:52:49.910 --> 00:52:51.270
have essentially camped out there.

1120
00:52:51.270 --> 00:52:53.310
They like it there and they're not getting off.

1121
00:52:53.310 --> 00:52:57.510
And that is what drives relapse even after people's lives

1122
00:52:57.510 --> 00:53:00.120
have gotten objectively better, right?

1123
00:53:00.120 --> 00:53:03.220
They've gotten their spouse back, their job back,

1124
00:53:03.220 --> 00:53:05.430
and then people see them relapsed and they say,

1125
00:53:05.430 --> 00:53:06.780
well, why would they do that?

1126
00:53:06.780 --> 00:53:08.370
Everything was going so well.

1127
00:53:08.370 --> 00:53:10.480
But if you put yourself in the mind of that person,

1128
00:53:10.480 --> 00:53:13.190
what you would see is that, every day they get up,

1129
00:53:13.190 --> 00:53:16.020
they are anxious, they are irritable, they are craving.

1130
00:53:16.020 --> 00:53:18.310
And that is what drives relapse.

1131
00:53:18.310 --> 00:53:23.060
It's sort of that, that intense physiologic and psychologic

1132
00:53:23.060 --> 00:53:24.013
suffering really.

1133
00:53:25.330 --> 00:53:27.340
Yeah. After the acute withdrawal,

1134
00:53:27.340 --> 00:53:30.829
that protracted period where everything just feels gray.

1135
00:53:30.829 --> 00:53:31.662
Yes.

1136
00:53:31.662 --> 00:53:33.794
Because you're so used to those dopamine hits.

1137
00:53:33.794 --> 00:53:34.627
Yes.

1138
00:53:34.627 --> 00:53:36.680
And even though your life is getting better

1139
00:53:36.680 --> 00:53:38.360
in the back of your mind, you're just like,

1140
00:53:38.360 --> 00:53:40.000
if I just do this one thing,

1141
00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:42.080
like I'll be able to write that paper

1142
00:53:42.080 --> 00:53:45.660
or get through this uncomfortable experience.

1143
00:53:45.660 --> 00:53:47.074
And I'm just gonna do it once.

1144
00:53:47.074 --> 00:53:47.907
Mm-hmm.

1145
00:53:47.907 --> 00:53:51.530
And that's the cunning baffling and powerful component

1146
00:53:51.530 --> 00:53:54.970
here that mystifies the non-addicts

1147
00:53:54.970 --> 00:53:58.010
because it leaves them just utterly confused

1148
00:53:58.010 --> 00:53:59.960
as to why somebody would make that choice.

1149
00:53:59.960 --> 00:54:04.663
But it's almost impossible to avoid depending upon-

1150
00:54:06.044 --> 00:54:07.940
Yes.

1151
00:54:07.940 --> 00:54:09.310
The behavior and the substance,

1152
00:54:09.310 --> 00:54:10.143
obviously some are more powerful-

1153
00:54:10.143 --> 00:54:13.730
And what I've come to appreciate is that something strange

1154
00:54:13.730 --> 00:54:17.590
happens to our perception of time when we're in that state.

1155
00:54:17.590 --> 00:54:20.440
So we're in that state of craving and dysphoria,

1156
00:54:20.440 --> 00:54:23.760
it really feels like it will never end.

1157
00:54:23.760 --> 00:54:24.680
I mean, it will,

1158
00:54:24.680 --> 00:54:28.380
you know, in most cases we know with sustained abstinence,

1159
00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:31.320
the gremlins hop off, homeostasis is restored,

1160
00:54:31.320 --> 00:54:33.420
but when we are in that state,

1161
00:54:33.420 --> 00:54:36.260
it feels as if it will go on forever.

1162
00:54:36.260 --> 00:54:40.110
Plus, as you said, we have a way to fix it, right?

1163
00:54:40.110 --> 00:54:41.390
It's right there within reach.

1164
00:54:41.390 --> 00:54:44.510
If we use again, we can relieve those feelings.

1165
00:54:44.510 --> 00:54:46.600
So I think that's the combination of those things

1166
00:54:46.600 --> 00:54:49.110
that the distorted time perception,

1167
00:54:49.110 --> 00:54:52.110
that those awful feelings will never end,

1168
00:54:52.110 --> 00:54:53.330
even though they will.

1169
00:54:53.330 --> 00:54:56.120
And knowing that we can make ourselves feel better

1170
00:54:56.120 --> 00:54:56.996
if we just-

1171
00:54:56.996 --> 00:54:59.470
Yeah. And if you're telling the patient they're facing

1172
00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:02.058
the prospect of possibly years of this.

1173
00:55:02.058 --> 00:55:02.891
Right.

1174
00:55:02.891 --> 00:55:04.220
I mean, if they're coming off benzos

1175
00:55:04.220 --> 00:55:07.140
or something like that, they're in for a very long,

1176
00:55:07.140 --> 00:55:07.973
hard road.

1177
00:55:07.973 --> 00:55:09.260
Yes, that's right.

1178
00:55:09.260 --> 00:55:13.110
Fortunately in my experience, most people who abstain

1179
00:55:13.110 --> 00:55:17.550
for one month begin to notice improvements in mood,

1180
00:55:17.550 --> 00:55:20.130
hopefulness, you know, sleep.

1181
00:55:20.130 --> 00:55:21.930
They might not be where they wanna be,

1182
00:55:21.930 --> 00:55:23.940
but they begin to see a little bit of light

1183
00:55:23.940 --> 00:55:24.960
at the end of the tunnel.

1184
00:55:24.960 --> 00:55:27.040
Not always, but that's the piece

1185
00:55:27.040 --> 00:55:29.070
that then I really have to remind them of.

1186
00:55:29.070 --> 00:55:32.190
And I say, remember how you felt when, you know,

1187
00:55:32.190 --> 00:55:35.070
and if you can just hang in there with recovery

1188
00:55:35.070 --> 00:55:38.810
and with abstinence, you know, incrementally in small ways,

1189
00:55:38.810 --> 00:55:40.180
you will get better.

1190
00:55:40.180 --> 00:55:43.140
And I think that's an important function that I serve.

1191
00:55:43.140 --> 00:55:45.650
Kind of a cheerleader and a reminder

1192
00:55:45.650 --> 00:55:47.820
because the hippocampus is tricky.

1193
00:55:47.820 --> 00:55:50.580
I also think that's a major function of AA, right?

1194
00:55:50.580 --> 00:55:53.760
That we, we go, I use the we pronoun.

1195
00:55:53.760 --> 00:55:56.350
So I'm, I'm not a member of AA, but in my clinical work,

1196
00:55:56.350 --> 00:55:57.790
I use the we pronoun because again,

1197
00:55:57.790 --> 00:56:00.170
I think we're all broken and humbled in the face

1198
00:56:00.170 --> 00:56:01.880
of this problem.

1199
00:56:01.880 --> 00:56:04.930
So I'll say to patients, you know,

1200
00:56:04.930 --> 00:56:07.150
remember how you felt then,

1201
00:56:07.150 --> 00:56:08.970
remember how you felt a little bit better,

1202
00:56:08.970 --> 00:56:10.623
you've done this before,

1203
00:56:11.697 --> 00:56:14.930
you have the data from recovery, hold that close

1204
00:56:14.930 --> 00:56:18.790
and tincture of time alone will get you there.

1205
00:56:18.790 --> 00:56:23.790
Are there cases where that dopamine balance never again,

1206
00:56:23.900 --> 00:56:26.720
reaches some level of homeostasis?

1207
00:56:26.720 --> 00:56:30.150
Yeah. So unfortunately I think that that can happen.

1208
00:56:30.150 --> 00:56:32.840
So for example, in the book that I think the case of Chris

1209
00:56:32.840 --> 00:56:34.860
possibly one of the things that happened to him

1210
00:56:34.860 --> 00:56:37.288
was after so many years of opioids, heroin-

1211
00:56:37.288 --> 00:56:38.360
Suboxone.

1212
00:56:38.360 --> 00:56:41.330
Yeah. That his balance was essentially broken,

1213
00:56:41.330 --> 00:56:43.150
it was stuck tilted to the side of pain.

1214
00:56:43.150 --> 00:56:45.770
And the only way for him to feel normal

1215
00:56:45.770 --> 00:56:49.000
is to be on what's called replacement therapy

1216
00:56:49.000 --> 00:56:52.310
or opioid agonist therapy in the form of Suboxone,

1217
00:56:52.310 --> 00:56:57.310
which has sustained him feeling well now going on

1218
00:56:57.600 --> 00:57:00.800
almost a decade and I've been talking very,

1219
00:57:00.800 --> 00:57:02.240
you know, he's doing great.

1220
00:57:02.240 --> 00:57:04.520
Its not like he's just kinda like tragic long,

1221
00:57:04.520 --> 00:57:05.560
he's doing great.

1222
00:57:05.560 --> 00:57:10.380
And he's been able to maintain that level of Suboxone

1223
00:57:10.380 --> 00:57:13.902
at a base rate, like he's not asking for more?

1224
00:57:13.902 --> 00:57:15.880
No. And I do see that.

1225
00:57:15.880 --> 00:57:18.050
I do have patients for whom,

1226
00:57:18.050 --> 00:57:19.460
for reasons we don't understand,

1227
00:57:19.460 --> 00:57:21.700
they do to develop tolerance, but not him.

1228
00:57:21.700 --> 00:57:24.330
It worked immediately, it worked well,

1229
00:57:24.330 --> 00:57:26.010
it restored homeostasis,

1230
00:57:26.010 --> 00:57:27.650
he's re-engaged with life,

1231
00:57:27.650 --> 00:57:29.030
and he's interesting to at least somebody

1232
00:57:29.030 --> 00:57:30.680
who never went to 12 step.

1233
00:57:30.680 --> 00:57:32.850
And never really got physychotherapy

1234
00:57:32.850 --> 00:57:37.850
beyond what we do, but that's what works for him.

1235
00:57:37.920 --> 00:57:39.920
Yeah. So interesting.

1236
00:57:39.920 --> 00:57:43.050
I'm always amazed by people who just figure out

1237
00:57:43.050 --> 00:57:46.740
how to get sober and stay sober without AA.

1238
00:57:46.740 --> 00:57:50.030
And, you know, I've often thought, what happens

1239
00:57:50.030 --> 00:57:52.710
if someone like yourself develop some kind of pill

1240
00:57:52.710 --> 00:57:56.920
that resolves, you know, alcoholism addiction

1241
00:57:56.920 --> 00:57:59.050
on a biochemical level, like what I take it

1242
00:57:59.050 --> 00:58:02.430
and I wouldn't trade my experiences or the richness

1243
00:58:02.430 --> 00:58:06.630
of my life experience and what I get out of this program

1244
00:58:06.630 --> 00:58:09.790
in this community for that option.

1245
00:58:09.790 --> 00:58:10.820
Yeah. That's so cool.

1246
00:58:10.820 --> 00:58:13.680
And I've heard that from so many of my patients that,

1247
00:58:13.680 --> 00:58:15.850
they get to a point in their recovery where they actually

1248
00:58:15.850 --> 00:58:18.413
regard their disease of addiction as a gift.

1249
00:58:19.400 --> 00:58:21.080
Yeah. I remember when I first came in

1250
00:58:21.080 --> 00:58:21.913
and you would hear it, you know,

1251
00:58:21.913 --> 00:58:24.770
there's always the guy who says, I'm a grateful alcoholic.

1252
00:58:24.770 --> 00:58:25.710
Yeah, right.

1253
00:58:25.710 --> 00:58:26.649
What kind of fucking asshole is that?

1254
00:58:26.649 --> 00:58:29.316
(both laughing)

1255
00:58:30.310 --> 00:58:31.240
Like how?

1256
00:58:31.240 --> 00:58:32.073
What?

1257
00:58:32.073 --> 00:58:33.280
You know, I didn't understand it.

1258
00:58:33.280 --> 00:58:35.677
Now of course, I completely understand that.

1259
00:58:35.677 --> 00:58:40.300
And that goes back to this idea of embracing

1260
00:58:40.300 --> 00:58:42.660
the painful parts of life and understanding

1261
00:58:42.660 --> 00:58:45.540
that those are our greatest teachers.

1262
00:58:45.540 --> 00:58:47.570
And if you can really learn about yourself

1263
00:58:47.570 --> 00:58:48.900
through those experiences,

1264
00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:51.100
you can create meaning around them.

1265
00:58:51.100 --> 00:58:53.220
That can be helpful for other people as well.

1266
00:58:53.220 --> 00:58:55.450
Yeah. I mean, as a physician, you know,

1267
00:58:55.450 --> 00:58:58.400
one of the things that attracted me to addiction medicine

1268
00:58:58.400 --> 00:59:00.310
in addition to the patients themselves,

1269
00:59:00.310 --> 00:59:03.950
was the docs in recovery who practice addiction medicine.

1270
00:59:03.950 --> 00:59:07.040
Because they're not like any other types of doctors.

1271
00:59:07.040 --> 00:59:08.910
You know, when I go to medical meetings

1272
00:59:08.910 --> 00:59:11.180
that are not addiction focused,

1273
00:59:11.180 --> 00:59:13.760
everybody's like trying to show off how much they know

1274
00:59:13.760 --> 00:59:16.680
more than the other guy, and then you go to, you know,

1275
00:59:16.680 --> 00:59:19.080
an addiction medicine conference and people are like,

1276
00:59:19.080 --> 00:59:21.780
yeah, you know I'm X number of years in recovery

1277
00:59:21.780 --> 00:59:24.190
and oh man, I'm struggling with this character defect

1278
00:59:24.190 --> 00:59:27.150
and that character defect and I'm so embarrassed by this

1279
00:59:27.150 --> 00:59:28.480
or that, or another thing I did,

1280
00:59:28.480 --> 00:59:30.090
it's just such a cool culture.

1281
00:59:30.090 --> 00:59:31.670
Yeah. And it's empowering.

1282
00:59:31.670 --> 00:59:32.503
Yeah.

1283
00:59:32.503 --> 00:59:34.220
I never get tired of hearing the stories.

1284
00:59:34.220 --> 00:59:36.180
And I'm sure when people read your book,

1285
00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:37.562
they're gonna be so shocked.

1286
00:59:37.562 --> 00:59:38.395
Yes.

1287
00:59:38.395 --> 00:59:40.520
To read these but, I'm so inculcated in that.

1288
00:59:40.520 --> 00:59:43.240
Like I'm so used to hearing the craziest stories

1289
00:59:43.240 --> 00:59:44.280
and I love it.

1290
00:59:44.280 --> 00:59:46.860
I love the honesty and the vulnerability of all of it.

1291
00:59:46.860 --> 00:59:48.480
And it's so empowering.

1292
00:59:48.480 --> 00:59:52.400
And it's such a beautiful expression of our shared humanity.

1293
00:59:52.400 --> 00:59:54.220
Yes, wonderfully put.

1294
00:59:54.220 --> 00:59:59.220
You know, when I first wrote the book and I gave it to,

1295
00:59:59.710 --> 01:00:04.710
you know, my agent and the editor, they were like, you know,

1296
01:00:05.260 --> 01:00:06.810
this story of sex addiction,

1297
01:00:06.810 --> 01:00:11.110
like maybe you could put it toward the middle or, you know,

1298
01:00:11.110 --> 01:00:12.100
at the back.

1299
01:00:12.100 --> 01:00:15.272
You're just blasting people from page one.

1300
01:00:15.272 --> 01:00:16.105
(Rich laughing)
Like,

1301
01:00:16.105 --> 01:00:17.550
they were really worried that just people

1302
01:00:17.550 --> 01:00:19.000
would just be so freaked out.

1303
01:00:19.000 --> 01:00:21.580
It would be so other that it just,

1304
01:00:21.580 --> 01:00:22.660
that nobody would read it.

1305
01:00:22.660 --> 01:00:24.140
And I'm like, you know what?

1306
01:00:24.140 --> 01:00:27.030
I'm not gonna do that because this is exactly

1307
01:00:27.030 --> 01:00:28.640
the point that I'm trying to make,

1308
01:00:28.640 --> 01:00:33.040
like pornography and sex addiction, it is everywhere.

1309
01:00:33.040 --> 01:00:35.750
I mean, we're not being truthful with ourselves

1310
01:00:35.750 --> 01:00:37.980
if we are pretending like this is not a huge problem,

1311
01:00:37.980 --> 01:00:39.350
this is a huge problem

1312
01:00:39.350 --> 01:00:41.220
and we need to put it up front and center.

1313
01:00:41.220 --> 01:00:43.020
Now the book is not just about sex addiction,

1314
01:00:43.020 --> 01:00:46.770
it's about all kinds of addictions, but like you,

1315
01:00:46.770 --> 01:00:48.230
I hear these stories every day.

1316
01:00:48.230 --> 01:00:51.100
So to me, they're not freaky and other,

1317
01:00:51.100 --> 01:00:54.540
and in the book, I really relate my patient to my own

1318
01:00:54.540 --> 01:00:57.530
kind of sex addiction that I developed.

1319
01:00:57.530 --> 01:00:59.770
And I wanted to do that, you know?

1320
01:00:59.770 --> 01:01:02.620
As you said, I wanted to make sure that people understand,

1321
01:01:02.620 --> 01:01:04.960
like, this is all of us, right?

1322
01:01:04.960 --> 01:01:07.982
I mean, this is our shared humanity as you said.

1323
01:01:07.982 --> 01:01:12.982
Yeah. I mean, a lot of people will remember you

1324
01:01:13.550 --> 01:01:15.364
from the social dilemma.

1325
01:01:15.364 --> 01:01:16.197
Right.

1326
01:01:16.197 --> 01:01:19.860
You appeared in that documentary and that's a beautiful,

1327
01:01:19.860 --> 01:01:22.430
you know, starting point to have this conversation

1328
01:01:22.430 --> 01:01:25.300
about the universality of all of this,

1329
01:01:25.300 --> 01:01:27.780
whether it's sex addiction or online shopping

1330
01:01:27.780 --> 01:01:32.670
or porn or gambling, or, you know, Twitch streaming,

1331
01:01:32.670 --> 01:01:33.503
it doesn't matter.

1332
01:01:33.503 --> 01:01:38.503
Like it's so ubiquitous and on some level we're all addicts

1333
01:01:39.130 --> 01:01:41.208
or we're all addicts in waiting.

1334
01:01:41.208 --> 01:01:42.260
Right.
Right?

1335
01:01:42.260 --> 01:01:44.350
And so, you know, I thought it was interesting

1336
01:01:44.350 --> 01:01:45.873
that in this book, you know,

1337
01:01:47.400 --> 01:01:49.590
the word addiction is not used on the cover.

1338
01:01:49.590 --> 01:01:52.000
Like this is not a book for addicts to read,

1339
01:01:52.000 --> 01:01:54.030
this is a book for everybody to read,

1340
01:01:54.030 --> 01:01:56.600
because I think it's important that we reframe

1341
01:01:56.600 --> 01:02:00.550
how we think about addiction and all of the levers

1342
01:02:00.550 --> 01:02:04.660
and pulleys and buttons that are out there right now,

1343
01:02:04.660 --> 01:02:07.240
just waiting to trigger us and lure us

1344
01:02:07.240 --> 01:02:11.140
into unhealthy relationships with substances

1345
01:02:11.140 --> 01:02:13.190
and things and behaviors and everything.

1346
01:02:13.190 --> 01:02:14.360
All of it out there.

1347
01:02:14.360 --> 01:02:15.430
Yes. Yep.

1348
01:02:15.430 --> 01:02:16.263
You get it.

1349
01:02:16.263 --> 01:02:17.720
Exactly.

1350
01:02:17.720 --> 01:02:20.500
So elaborate, I guess.

1351
01:02:20.500 --> 01:02:23.423
I don't know that wasn't really a question, but you know,

1352
01:02:23.423 --> 01:02:25.860
this idea that we are all addicts,

1353
01:02:25.860 --> 01:02:27.430
I think is regulatory for it.

1354
01:02:27.430 --> 01:02:31.122
And I think people that may ruffle some feathers.

1355
01:02:31.122 --> 01:02:33.470
Like I'm not that, like, I just know,

1356
01:02:33.470 --> 01:02:35.830
even when I was in the depths of alcoholism,

1357
01:02:35.830 --> 01:02:38.060
I would always look at people who were worse than me

1358
01:02:38.060 --> 01:02:42.070
to say that I'm not this, as opposed to looking for ways

1359
01:02:42.070 --> 01:02:44.780
to identify or find similarities.

1360
01:02:44.780 --> 01:02:47.810
Yeah. I mean, if you look at the basic wiring

1361
01:02:47.810 --> 01:02:50.980
of the brain, we're all wired to approach pleasure

1362
01:02:50.980 --> 01:02:52.020
and avoid pain.

1363
01:02:52.020 --> 01:02:54.920
And that is what has kept us alive in a world of scarcity

1364
01:02:54.920 --> 01:02:58.100
for, you know, gazillions of years.

1365
01:02:58.100 --> 01:03:00.900
And the fundamental problem now is that we are not living

1366
01:03:00.900 --> 01:03:01.970
in that world of scarcity.

1367
01:03:01.970 --> 01:03:04.140
We live in this world of overwhelming abundance,

1368
01:03:04.140 --> 01:03:08.460
but our brain chemistry and our wiring hasn't changed.

1369
01:03:08.460 --> 01:03:11.700
So when you are basically wired to seek out pleasure

1370
01:03:11.700 --> 01:03:14.700
and avoid pain, and you live in a world

1371
01:03:14.700 --> 01:03:18.290
surrounded by pleasure goods, infinitely available

1372
01:03:18.290 --> 01:03:22.410
at the tap of a finger, and you're encouraged to avoid pain

1373
01:03:22.410 --> 01:03:25.300
at all costs, you know, how could we not get addicted?

1374
01:03:25.300 --> 01:03:28.250
Of course we are, and we will.

1375
01:03:28.250 --> 01:03:31.960
And I do think people are beginning to relate to that,

1376
01:03:31.960 --> 01:03:35.660
especially as regards smartphones and things

1377
01:03:35.660 --> 01:03:38.770
that we're doing online, because people are now observing

1378
01:03:38.770 --> 01:03:41.330
their own behavior, getting, you know, losing time,

1379
01:03:41.330 --> 01:03:44.770
getting caught up, spending way more time than they planned.

1380
01:03:44.770 --> 01:03:48.210
Having it interfere with their parenting or their work,

1381
01:03:48.210 --> 01:03:50.010
or so I do think that there,

1382
01:03:50.010 --> 01:03:52.860
I hope that there is sort of this dawning awareness

1383
01:03:52.860 --> 01:03:55.407
that this is a universal phenomenon,

1384
01:03:55.407 --> 01:03:59.300
and that yes, we're variably vulnerable, which is to say,

1385
01:03:59.300 --> 01:04:02.180
we're not all equally gonna, you know, become addicted,

1386
01:04:02.180 --> 01:04:04.220
but that we are all vulnerable

1387
01:04:04.220 --> 01:04:06.483
to the fundamental problem now.

1388
01:04:07.410 --> 01:04:10.632
And that were essentially outgunned and outmatched.

1389
01:04:10.632 --> 01:04:11.465
Yes.

1390
01:04:11.465 --> 01:04:14.120
You know, that was a big point in the documentary that,

1391
01:04:14.120 --> 01:04:16.620
you think that you have some agency here.

1392
01:04:16.620 --> 01:04:19.490
Well, think again, because you have no idea how much money

1393
01:04:19.490 --> 01:04:22.850
and science has gone into removing that agency

1394
01:04:22.850 --> 01:04:25.270
when it comes to your relationship with your devices.

1395
01:04:25.270 --> 01:04:26.410
Yes, exactly.

1396
01:04:26.410 --> 01:04:28.700
And also importantly, and this is, again,

1397
01:04:28.700 --> 01:04:30.250
something I learned from my patients,

1398
01:04:30.250 --> 01:04:32.360
when we are in our addictions,

1399
01:04:32.360 --> 01:04:36.480
we cannot see the true consequences of those behaviors.

1400
01:04:36.480 --> 01:04:38.030
I mean, you know, in the world of addiction,

1401
01:04:38.030 --> 01:04:39.590
it's called denial, but I mean,

1402
01:04:39.590 --> 01:04:41.650
it's really a fascinating phenomenon,

1403
01:04:41.650 --> 01:04:45.800
how we cannot objectively observe ourselves

1404
01:04:45.800 --> 01:04:49.090
or the consequences of these compulsive behaviors

1405
01:04:49.090 --> 01:04:51.980
until we get some distance from it and look back

1406
01:04:51.980 --> 01:04:54.300
and then kind of go, wow, that was surreal

1407
01:04:54.300 --> 01:04:56.970
that I did that much of that thing for that long.

1408
01:04:56.970 --> 01:04:58.640
Like, that was really bizarre.

1409
01:04:58.640 --> 01:05:01.230
And I also lied about it and like, you know,

1410
01:05:01.230 --> 01:05:03.960
did all these like fancy maneuvers to do it more.

1411
01:05:03.960 --> 01:05:05.123
And now I look back and it's like,

1412
01:05:05.123 --> 01:05:06.737
I've had so many of my patients say,

1413
01:05:06.737 --> 01:05:08.736
"it's like another person,

1414
01:05:08.736 --> 01:05:10.330
it's like that was another person,"

1415
01:05:10.330 --> 01:05:12.570
you know, which is very interesting.

1416
01:05:12.570 --> 01:05:17.470
Yeah. Why did I sit on the toilet for an hour staring

1417
01:05:17.470 --> 01:05:18.303
at my phone,

1418
01:05:18.303 --> 01:05:20.370
(all laughing)
you know?

1419
01:05:20.370 --> 01:05:22.600
There are a lot of teenagers out there

1420
01:05:22.600 --> 01:05:24.630
spending a lot of time in the bathrooms these days,

1421
01:05:24.630 --> 01:05:25.470
I can tell you that.

1422
01:05:25.470 --> 01:05:26.470
Listen, I've done it.

1423
01:05:26.470 --> 01:05:29.380
Like, I'll call myself out right now on that.

1424
01:05:29.380 --> 01:05:32.460
When you were talking about the doctors

1425
01:05:33.720 --> 01:05:36.950
that are your colleagues that are in recovery,

1426
01:05:36.950 --> 01:05:41.513
it reminded me of my experience in treatment.

1427
01:05:42.950 --> 01:05:44.830
I went to a treatment center where,

1428
01:05:44.830 --> 01:05:48.760
there was a lot of professionals there on diversion.

1429
01:05:48.760 --> 01:05:51.860
So a lot of doctors, and a lot of pilots,

1430
01:05:51.860 --> 01:05:53.620
like the two people that you literally

1431
01:05:53.620 --> 01:05:55.693
give all of your agency over to,

1432
01:05:56.556 --> 01:05:59.340
it's very horrifying to realize that surgeons

1433
01:05:59.340 --> 01:06:02.500
and anesthesiologists and commercial airline pilots

1434
01:06:02.500 --> 01:06:03.880
were in treatment.

1435
01:06:03.880 --> 01:06:06.040
And I wanted to share anonymously,

1436
01:06:06.040 --> 01:06:08.047
some of those stories when I was writing,

1437
01:06:08.047 --> 01:06:10.680
"Finding Ultra" and my publisher said the same thing.

1438
01:06:10.680 --> 01:06:12.576
They're like, it's too crazy, you can't do that.

1439
01:06:12.576 --> 01:06:13.409
(Anna laughing)

1440
01:06:13.409 --> 01:06:18.310
But to hear these, you know, tales from anesthesiologists

1441
01:06:18.310 --> 01:06:22.380
like stealing fentanyl and how it made them more productive

1442
01:06:22.380 --> 01:06:24.500
leads to ketamine and jumping off roofs

1443
01:06:24.500 --> 01:06:25.810
and all kinds of crazy stuff.

1444
01:06:25.810 --> 01:06:28.750
Like there was a doctor who was taking just handfuls

1445
01:06:28.750 --> 01:06:32.640
of Vicodin every day there was another neurosurgeon

1446
01:06:32.640 --> 01:06:37.370
who was a IV morphine addict, like, you know, it's wild.

1447
01:06:37.370 --> 01:06:40.040
You know, being smart and being highly educated

1448
01:06:40.040 --> 01:06:42.650
is not any protection against addiction

1449
01:06:42.650 --> 01:06:44.460
and might even make you more vulnerable

1450
01:06:44.460 --> 01:06:46.226
because you think that you'll know.

1451
01:06:46.226 --> 01:06:47.100
You're superman.

1452
01:06:47.100 --> 01:06:50.160
Yeah, or that you'll know when you've crossed the line.

1453
01:06:50.160 --> 01:06:53.940
I mean, you know, I've treated many docs in addiction

1454
01:06:53.940 --> 01:06:56.560
over the years, but the one that comes to mind now

1455
01:06:56.560 --> 01:06:57.720
as an anesthesiologist,

1456
01:06:57.720 --> 01:06:59.870
you know, who had just a big stash of pills,

1457
01:06:59.870 --> 01:07:02.970
that he would take them in all different combinations

1458
01:07:02.970 --> 01:07:03.803
and permutations.

1459
01:07:03.803 --> 01:07:05.850
And he just thought, well, I'm an anesthesiologist,

1460
01:07:05.850 --> 01:07:09.130
you know, so I know how to dose this stuff until he didn't,

1461
01:07:09.130 --> 01:07:09.963
you know what I mean?

1462
01:07:09.963 --> 01:07:12.520
There's gotta be a higher percentage of anesthesiologists

1463
01:07:12.520 --> 01:07:14.650
that become addicts versus other doctors.

1464
01:07:14.650 --> 01:07:17.750
Yes, the higher percentage of anesthesiologists

1465
01:07:17.750 --> 01:07:18.869
and psychiatry.

1466
01:07:18.869 --> 01:07:20.040
Mm-mmh.

1467
01:07:20.040 --> 01:07:21.780
Why psychiatrist?

1468
01:07:21.780 --> 01:07:23.740
Ah, you know, we're kind of stuck in our heads.

1469
01:07:23.740 --> 01:07:26.920
Is it the brokenness that allures you into the profession

1470
01:07:26.920 --> 01:07:28.390
to begin with?

1471
01:07:28.390 --> 01:07:30.983
I think it's the kind of, I mean,

1472
01:07:30.983 --> 01:07:34.490
I wouldn't wanna speak for all psychiatrists,

1473
01:07:34.490 --> 01:07:36.460
but I don't know.

1474
01:07:36.460 --> 01:07:37.293
I don't know what it is.

1475
01:07:37.293 --> 01:07:39.040
I mean, many people who go into psychiatry

1476
01:07:39.040 --> 01:07:40.820
have family members with mental illness

1477
01:07:40.820 --> 01:07:43.400
and so there's kind of a mission driven purpose

1478
01:07:43.400 --> 01:07:45.310
wanting to help others.

1479
01:07:45.310 --> 01:07:48.120
Sometimes it's being broken and wanting to help yourself,

1480
01:07:48.120 --> 01:07:49.980
but I think that's become less and less true

1481
01:07:49.980 --> 01:07:53.560
as the field has attracted a lot of very high-powered

1482
01:07:53.560 --> 01:07:56.670
students who are really into neuroscience and such.

1483
01:07:56.670 --> 01:07:58.100
I don't know, I don't know what it is.

1484
01:07:58.100 --> 01:08:00.450
I mean, with anesthesiologists,

1485
01:08:00.450 --> 01:08:04.340
you know, the assumption is it because of increased access,

1486
01:08:04.340 --> 01:08:07.223
increased access to things like opioids and Benzos.

1487
01:08:08.090 --> 01:08:10.570
But I think there's probably a self-selection process

1488
01:08:10.570 --> 01:08:12.730
where you've got people who are already maybe addicted

1489
01:08:12.730 --> 01:08:15.170
or vulnerable or almost addicted

1490
01:08:15.170 --> 01:08:17.780
who then subconsciously choose anesthesia

1491
01:08:17.780 --> 01:08:20.560
'cause maybe because of the access, I don't know.

1492
01:08:20.560 --> 01:08:21.560
Yeah.

1493
01:08:21.560 --> 01:08:23.610
Yeah. And you're in anesthesiology,

1494
01:08:23.610 --> 01:08:25.940
you're just dealing with those all the time.

1495
01:08:25.940 --> 01:08:29.740
And I hear these crazy, like you get these tiny little vials

1496
01:08:29.740 --> 01:08:32.690
of fentanyl and they would like take a little bit of it

1497
01:08:32.690 --> 01:08:34.240
and fill the rest of it with water

1498
01:08:34.240 --> 01:08:38.230
and the lengths that they would go to be undetected

1499
01:08:38.230 --> 01:08:41.660
and tapping into the pharma closet.

1500
01:08:41.660 --> 01:08:45.260
But, you know, I mean, doctors are just regular people

1501
01:08:45.260 --> 01:08:47.940
with all the same regular problems.

1502
01:08:47.940 --> 01:08:51.627
I mean, in some ways what's so tragic about doctors

1503
01:08:51.627 --> 01:08:54.100
and addiction and the problems of getting into recovery

1504
01:08:54.100 --> 01:08:56.040
when you're in that profession is because,

1505
01:08:56.040 --> 01:08:59.030
I think the shame is more pronounced because you're supposed

1506
01:08:59.030 --> 01:09:01.630
to be this healer, who's got it all together.

1507
01:09:01.630 --> 01:09:05.387
One of the things that was kind of scary for me in writing

1508
01:09:05.387 --> 01:09:09.460
"Dopamine Nation" was that obviously I disclose, you know,

1509
01:09:09.460 --> 01:09:11.630
ways that I'm kinda messed up.

1510
01:09:11.630 --> 01:09:15.380
So, I mean, that has brought up a lot of shame for me

1511
01:09:15.380 --> 01:09:17.720
and kind of worrying what other people will think,

1512
01:09:17.720 --> 01:09:21.250
it is fascinating how afraid we are to do that.

1513
01:09:21.250 --> 01:09:23.150
I think everybody's afraid to do that,

1514
01:09:23.150 --> 01:09:26.950
but I wonder if physicians are maybe a little more afraid.

1515
01:09:26.950 --> 01:09:28.050
Yeah, yeah.

1516
01:09:28.050 --> 01:09:30.120
I would suspect that that's true.

1517
01:09:30.120 --> 01:09:34.050
But there has to be a cathartic aspect

1518
01:09:34.050 --> 01:09:35.720
to that for you as well, right?

1519
01:09:35.720 --> 01:09:36.690
A freeing.

1520
01:09:36.690 --> 01:09:38.360
Yes, absolutely.

1521
01:09:38.360 --> 01:09:41.070
I mean, I really try to live a transparent life

1522
01:09:41.070 --> 01:09:43.170
where everything I say and do,

1523
01:09:43.170 --> 01:09:46.190
if it were published on the front page of a major newspaper,

1524
01:09:46.190 --> 01:09:47.543
I would be okay with it.

1525
01:09:48.434 --> 01:09:53.300
And so it's not really, maybe as much of a leap for me

1526
01:09:53.300 --> 01:09:58.210
to disclose those things as it would be for somebody,

1527
01:09:58.210 --> 01:10:00.450
you know, somebody in another field of medicine

1528
01:10:00.450 --> 01:10:03.150
or somebody with a different path than I've had.

1529
01:10:03.150 --> 01:10:05.890
But, you know, I mean, I've never read a doctor

1530
01:10:05.890 --> 01:10:08.020
authored book where they're nothing

1531
01:10:08.020 --> 01:10:09.890
but the most wonderful healer.

1532
01:10:09.890 --> 01:10:11.570
Do you know what I mean?
Right, right.

1533
01:10:11.570 --> 01:10:13.460
And that leaves you distrustful.

1534
01:10:13.460 --> 01:10:14.293
Yeah.

1535
01:10:14.293 --> 01:10:16.120
There's something about leading with vulnerability

1536
01:10:16.120 --> 01:10:19.420
and the honesty, you know, incumbent in that,

1537
01:10:19.420 --> 01:10:22.680
that leads me to, you know, feel like this person

1538
01:10:22.680 --> 01:10:24.240
has more integrity.

1539
01:10:24.240 --> 01:10:27.570
It works counter-intuitively to the way you think it might.

1540
01:10:27.570 --> 01:10:30.140
Yes. Well, I'm glad to hear that and I agree with you.

1541
01:10:30.140 --> 01:10:32.910
And in my own psychiatric practice

1542
01:10:32.910 --> 01:10:36.860
I've really changed the way that I viewed disclosure.

1543
01:10:36.860 --> 01:10:40.570
So we are trained to not disclose anything about ourselves

1544
01:10:40.570 --> 01:10:43.860
to be sort of that removed Freudian type of person

1545
01:10:43.860 --> 01:10:48.860
who, you know, listens empathically and strategizes

1546
01:10:48.910 --> 01:10:50.120
with the patient about how to get well,

1547
01:10:50.120 --> 01:10:52.850
but doesn't actually disclose our own thing,

1548
01:10:52.850 --> 01:10:54.850
but I'm not sure I believe that anymore.

1549
01:10:54.850 --> 01:10:57.710
First of all, I mean, our patients can see through us,

1550
01:10:57.710 --> 01:11:00.950
we bring ourselves to the practice,

1551
01:11:00.950 --> 01:11:03.700
but also I think that it's helpful for patients

1552
01:11:03.700 --> 01:11:06.357
to know that like we're all broken, you know,

1553
01:11:06.357 --> 01:11:07.700
and we all struggle.

1554
01:11:07.700 --> 01:11:10.160
And so I try to do it thoughtfully.

1555
01:11:10.160 --> 01:11:12.570
I'm not like airing my dirty laundry with my patients,

1556
01:11:12.570 --> 01:11:14.430
you know, going and on about my problems,

1557
01:11:14.430 --> 01:11:18.920
but I do strategically talk about my own anxiety, you know,

1558
01:11:18.920 --> 01:11:22.100
my own depression, my own insomnia,

1559
01:11:22.100 --> 01:11:24.850
my own sense of life being, you know,

1560
01:11:24.850 --> 01:11:27.673
kind of drudgery at times.

1561
01:11:27.673 --> 01:11:28.970
That is interesting, yeah.

1562
01:11:28.970 --> 01:11:31.700
Because you think of the therapeutic context

1563
01:11:31.700 --> 01:11:34.130
as you're this blank slate, right.

1564
01:11:34.130 --> 01:11:38.640
Who never wants, gives any clue as to what you think

1565
01:11:38.640 --> 01:11:39.550
or who you are.

1566
01:11:39.550 --> 01:11:40.540
Right. Right.

1567
01:11:40.540 --> 01:11:43.660
And so funny because the expressions on my patients' faces,

1568
01:11:43.660 --> 01:11:45.220
like when I do that,

1569
01:11:45.220 --> 01:11:47.070
initially there's sorta like this raised eyebrow,

1570
01:11:47.070 --> 01:11:49.690
like, get me out of here, get me out of here.

1571
01:11:49.690 --> 01:11:51.090
This lady is crazy.

1572
01:11:51.090 --> 01:11:52.940
Or like, this is about me, not you.

1573
01:11:53.820 --> 01:11:56.120
I don't get that so much because I don't go on and on,

1574
01:11:56.120 --> 01:11:57.680
but there's a sort of like, there's this initial,

1575
01:11:57.680 --> 01:11:59.840
this alarm is like, I don't wanna be treated

1576
01:11:59.840 --> 01:12:03.000
by a crazy psychiatrist, but then I think on some core

1577
01:12:03.000 --> 01:12:05.940
level, they recognize that, well, maybe she's,

1578
01:12:05.940 --> 01:12:07.600
you know, not that crazy

1579
01:12:07.600 --> 01:12:09.650
and maybe there's a reason that she's telling me that.

1580
01:12:09.650 --> 01:12:11.950
Although I did have this one patient who was telling me

1581
01:12:11.950 --> 01:12:14.710
about his flying phobia and I'm like, oh yeah, I get that.

1582
01:12:14.710 --> 01:12:15.570
And then he goes, "what?

1583
01:12:15.570 --> 01:12:16.870
I like to sit to the next to the window

1584
01:12:16.870 --> 01:12:19.180
'cause I feel like if I can see the ground somehow,

1585
01:12:19.180 --> 01:12:21.390
that makes it less likely that we're actually gonna fall."

1586
01:12:21.390 --> 01:12:22.700
And I'm like, I'm right with you there.

1587
01:12:22.700 --> 01:12:24.910
I always get the window seat.

1588
01:12:24.910 --> 01:12:26.827
And that's when he was like, "crazy."

1589
01:12:26.827 --> 01:12:28.680
(both laughing)

1590
01:12:28.680 --> 01:12:31.940
But he came back, he came back and he managed to get off

1591
01:12:31.940 --> 01:12:32.860
his benzodiazepine.

1592
01:12:32.860 --> 01:12:35.970
So, it was a success story.

1593
01:12:35.970 --> 01:12:40.803
Where are we in terms of the number of people that qualify

1594
01:12:43.600 --> 01:12:47.080
as being addicted maybe specifically to substances

1595
01:12:47.080 --> 01:12:49.090
right now, like it's on the rise, right?

1596
01:12:49.090 --> 01:12:52.640
There's a crazy spike in the percentage of people

1597
01:12:52.640 --> 01:12:55.430
that are dealing with some form of substance addiction.

1598
01:12:55.430 --> 01:12:58.790
Yeah. So, I mean, here's a crazy statistic.

1599
01:12:58.790 --> 01:13:03.790
50% of the world's deaths attributable to addiction

1600
01:13:04.440 --> 01:13:07.310
occur in those under age 50.

1601
01:13:07.310 --> 01:13:11.100
So, I mean, that's a whole lot of people, right?

1602
01:13:11.100 --> 01:13:14.550
If we've got more than half of the world's deaths

1603
01:13:14.550 --> 01:13:18.581
due to addiction in people under 50, that's kind of scary.

1604
01:13:18.581 --> 01:13:19.414
Wow.

1605
01:13:19.414 --> 01:13:24.380
And then if you look at specifically things like alcohol,

1606
01:13:24.380 --> 01:13:29.020
so, rates of alcohol have gone up in the last 20 years,

1607
01:13:29.020 --> 01:13:31.700
including in groups that were previously immune

1608
01:13:31.700 --> 01:13:35.610
to alcohol addiction, rates of alcohol use disorder,

1609
01:13:35.610 --> 01:13:40.610
alcohol addiction have gone up 50% in people over age 65.

1610
01:13:40.830 --> 01:13:42.150
From when to when?

1611
01:13:42.150 --> 01:13:46.200
Between the late 1990s and today.

1612
01:13:46.200 --> 01:13:49.050
And they've gone up 80% in women,

1613
01:13:49.050 --> 01:13:50.689
which is a really fascinating change.

1614
01:13:50.689 --> 01:13:53.200
Wow, so what do you make of that?

1615
01:13:53.200 --> 01:13:55.140
Well, it's complicated.

1616
01:13:55.140 --> 01:13:56.610
First of all, for older people,

1617
01:13:56.610 --> 01:13:59.600
what I make of it is that we are living longer, right?

1618
01:13:59.600 --> 01:14:03.070
And that as people age and their brains age,

1619
01:14:03.070 --> 01:14:06.040
a lot of folks who have been able to kind of moderate

1620
01:14:06.040 --> 01:14:08.520
their use for most of their middle years,

1621
01:14:08.520 --> 01:14:11.200
are finding in their latter years

1622
01:14:11.200 --> 01:14:14.810
that something changes biochemically, psychologically,

1623
01:14:14.810 --> 01:14:16.530
and all of a sudden they pop off into addiction.

1624
01:14:16.530 --> 01:14:18.250
I see these people all the time.

1625
01:14:18.250 --> 01:14:19.887
People, you know, basically like baby boomers, right?

1626
01:14:19.887 --> 01:14:22.830
It was like, I have smoked pot every day, since I was 25,

1627
01:14:22.830 --> 01:14:23.910
I never had a problem.

1628
01:14:23.910 --> 01:14:26.270
All of a sudden I'm dabbing, right?

1629
01:14:26.270 --> 01:14:28.290
And it's unmanageable.

1630
01:14:28.290 --> 01:14:33.000
And in some ways harder because now you have, you know,

1631
01:14:33.000 --> 01:14:36.140
65 years of habit and learned experience

1632
01:14:36.140 --> 01:14:38.640
around using every day, you're gonna give that up

1633
01:14:38.640 --> 01:14:42.800
when you're 65, 70, you have less brain plasticity

1634
01:14:42.800 --> 01:14:44.210
to form new habits.

1635
01:14:44.210 --> 01:14:47.060
So I think the older people phenomenon is both a function

1636
01:14:47.060 --> 01:14:51.160
of just living longer, having more time, maybe more boredom.

1637
01:14:51.160 --> 01:14:54.670
And then also the more potent drugs that we have now,

1638
01:14:54.670 --> 01:14:57.120
the more variety people slipping into addiction

1639
01:14:57.120 --> 01:14:58.050
in older age.

1640
01:14:58.050 --> 01:15:01.440
For women, it's really fascinating because, I mean,

1641
01:15:01.440 --> 01:15:04.260
I see that all the time where, you know,

1642
01:15:04.260 --> 01:15:06.900
traditionally the rates of addiction of men to women,

1643
01:15:06.900 --> 01:15:09.090
the ratio has been like 5:1,

1644
01:15:09.090 --> 01:15:11.560
five men to every one woman with addiction.

1645
01:15:11.560 --> 01:15:14.360
Now it's one to one in millennials.

1646
01:15:14.360 --> 01:15:16.542
I mean, it's like equal amounts.

1647
01:15:16.542 --> 01:15:17.375
Wow.

1648
01:15:17.375 --> 01:15:20.307
And I mean, I could speculate on why that is.

1649
01:15:20.307 --> 01:15:22.740
I guess I will speculate.

1650
01:15:22.740 --> 01:15:26.060
I think that, you know, with the women's movement

1651
01:15:26.060 --> 01:15:30.290
and you know, more opportunity and more equality,

1652
01:15:30.290 --> 01:15:32.013
I think there are trade offs, right?

1653
01:15:32.013 --> 01:15:35.960
That the burden that comes with with burdens

1654
01:15:35.960 --> 01:15:40.960
that may be leading women into more addictive tendencies,

1655
01:15:41.170 --> 01:15:43.217
plus you've got more potency and more variety.

1656
01:15:43.217 --> 01:15:44.050
Right, yeah.

1657
01:15:44.050 --> 01:15:46.890
That's what came to mind for me immediately.

1658
01:15:46.890 --> 01:15:51.190
Like that just the proliferation of so many different drugs

1659
01:15:51.190 --> 01:15:54.170
now and the potency of that.

1660
01:15:54.170 --> 01:15:55.340
Yes, right.

1661
01:15:55.340 --> 01:15:57.300
Compared to what it was decades.

1662
01:15:57.300 --> 01:15:58.270
That's a big part of it.

1663
01:15:58.270 --> 01:16:01.060
But I also think part of is, you know, culturally,

1664
01:16:01.060 --> 01:16:05.280
like women weren't really supposed to imbibe

1665
01:16:05.280 --> 01:16:08.549
and now, like that's not really...

1666
01:16:08.549 --> 01:16:11.530
That was cultural morals aren't really there anymore.

1667
01:16:11.530 --> 01:16:13.700
You know, for better and for worse.

1668
01:16:13.700 --> 01:16:14.750
Yeah.

1669
01:16:14.750 --> 01:16:15.583
That's interesting.

1670
01:16:15.583 --> 01:16:20.470
And how does that break down in terms of opioid addiction?

1671
01:16:22.140 --> 01:16:26.150
Like what percentage of that can be attributed to opioids?

1672
01:16:26.150 --> 01:16:28.970
In terms of addiction to opioids,

1673
01:16:28.970 --> 01:16:33.190
I don't know if we have consistent science on that.

1674
01:16:33.190 --> 01:16:37.170
You know, some studies show that women are more likely

1675
01:16:37.170 --> 01:16:41.210
to be addicted to opioids, other studies show men.

1676
01:16:41.210 --> 01:16:42.950
One thing we know for sure is that men

1677
01:16:42.950 --> 01:16:45.190
are more likely to die from opioids.

1678
01:16:45.190 --> 01:16:48.010
And that's sort of interesting and not entirely clear

1679
01:16:48.010 --> 01:16:49.790
why that is.

1680
01:16:49.790 --> 01:16:53.920
But I think in general, the rates break down to about 50/50.

1681
01:16:53.920 --> 01:16:58.250
Right. And then in just in terms of addiction at large,

1682
01:16:58.250 --> 01:17:00.140
like in this spike that we're seeing

1683
01:17:00.140 --> 01:17:04.050
in the rates of addiction, how much of that overall,

1684
01:17:04.050 --> 01:17:07.103
what portion of that is opioid related?

1685
01:17:08.120 --> 01:17:12.000
Well, if you look at the current drug overdose deaths,

1686
01:17:12.000 --> 01:17:14.740
the majority involve some kind of opioid.

1687
01:17:14.740 --> 01:17:17.830
Polypharmacy is the norm in drug overdose deaths

1688
01:17:17.830 --> 01:17:21.440
and in fact, what confers a lot of the risk is polypharmacy.

1689
01:17:21.440 --> 01:17:24.160
So mixing a bunch of drugs together as a lot more dangerous

1690
01:17:24.160 --> 01:17:26.860
than monotherapy, but a lot of times people are taking

1691
01:17:26.860 --> 01:17:28.890
a mixture of drugs and don't know it, right?

1692
01:17:28.890 --> 01:17:31.870
Because a counterfeit pill that they think is a Xanax bar

1693
01:17:31.870 --> 01:17:36.470
actually has Flualprazolam, benzodiazepine, designer drug,

1694
01:17:36.470 --> 01:17:37.730
plus a little bit of fentanyl in it.

1695
01:17:37.730 --> 01:17:38.947
And they don't know that that's what they're taking.

1696
01:17:38.947 --> 01:17:40.697
Mm-hmm. Wow.

1697
01:17:42.300 --> 01:17:44.230
So, what did you ask me?

1698
01:17:44.230 --> 01:17:45.352
Sorry.
(Anne chuckling)

1699
01:17:45.352 --> 01:17:48.120
I'm just trying to get at, I wanna kind of segue

1700
01:17:48.120 --> 01:17:50.950
into the whole opioid crisis

1701
01:17:50.950 --> 01:17:53.960
'cause this is obviously an area of expertise for you

1702
01:17:53.960 --> 01:17:57.710
and as a foundational to that, like just getting a sense

1703
01:17:57.710 --> 01:18:01.130
of how big the opioid problem is and then what percentage

1704
01:18:01.130 --> 01:18:04.220
of addiction overall it kind of commandeers.

1705
01:18:04.220 --> 01:18:08.490
Okay. So there's somewhere between two and 15 million

1706
01:18:08.490 --> 01:18:11.310
Americans today addicted to opioids.

1707
01:18:11.310 --> 01:18:13.700
Why is the range so large?

1708
01:18:13.700 --> 01:18:18.030
Because the way that we do those surveys varies a lot.

1709
01:18:18.030 --> 01:18:20.390
According to the National Survey on Drug Use and Health

1710
01:18:20.390 --> 01:18:23.370
it's about 2 million Americans addicted to opioids

1711
01:18:23.370 --> 01:18:26.260
with about 11 to 12 million Americans,

1712
01:18:26.260 --> 01:18:28.720
misusing prescription opioids.

1713
01:18:28.720 --> 01:18:32.790
So, slightly different misuse is not necessarily addiction.

1714
01:18:32.790 --> 01:18:36.770
Addiction is crossing into kind of a crossing a threshold

1715
01:18:36.770 --> 01:18:41.250
there, so, but on the outer range, you know,

1716
01:18:41.250 --> 01:18:43.330
some studies have included, for example,

1717
01:18:43.330 --> 01:18:46.390
homeless populations or incarcerated populations

1718
01:18:46.390 --> 01:18:49.320
have gotten up to as high as 15 million Americans

1719
01:18:49.320 --> 01:18:50.310
addicted to opioids.

1720
01:18:50.310 --> 01:18:55.130
So, it's, by any count, millions of Americans

1721
01:18:55.130 --> 01:18:58.880
either addicted to and, or misusing opioids.

1722
01:18:58.880 --> 01:19:02.000
And then if you look at opioid related overdose deaths,

1723
01:19:02.000 --> 01:19:04.680
what you see is that they've essentially been rising

1724
01:19:04.680 --> 01:19:07.310
steadily since the late 1990s.

1725
01:19:07.310 --> 01:19:10.930
They seem to go down and plateau a little bit in 2018,

1726
01:19:10.930 --> 01:19:14.070
but in 2020 they rose the biggest percentage

1727
01:19:14.070 --> 01:19:16.353
they have in the last 20 years.

1728
01:19:16.353 --> 01:19:17.990
Is that pandemic related?

1729
01:19:17.990 --> 01:19:21.020
Yeah. We think it's pandemic related.

1730
01:19:21.020 --> 01:19:26.020
A combination of an ongoing potent drug supply especially,

1731
01:19:26.550 --> 01:19:29.820
including fentanyl, which is 50-100 times more potent

1732
01:19:29.820 --> 01:19:34.200
than morphine or heroin combined with increased isolation

1733
01:19:34.200 --> 01:19:35.870
and decreased access to treatment.

1734
01:19:35.870 --> 01:19:36.720
Right.

1735
01:19:36.720 --> 01:19:37.940
So wild.

1736
01:19:37.940 --> 01:19:39.150
Yeah.

1737
01:19:39.150 --> 01:19:41.120
So let's talk about how we got here.

1738
01:19:41.120 --> 01:19:43.890
I mean, you wrote this other book, "Drug Dealer MD,"

1739
01:19:43.890 --> 01:19:48.040
it's a pretty fascinating dissection of the origins

1740
01:19:48.040 --> 01:19:51.010
of the opioid crisis, how we got here

1741
01:19:51.010 --> 01:19:54.993
and the multiple contributing factors to it.

1742
01:19:56.980 --> 01:20:01.800
Talk a little bit about that, the idea of big pharma

1743
01:20:01.800 --> 01:20:04.880
and big medicine being in cahoots to, you know,

1744
01:20:04.880 --> 01:20:07.590
create this problem that we're having

1745
01:20:07.590 --> 01:20:10.430
such difficulty figuring out.

1746
01:20:10.430 --> 01:20:12.920
Yeah. So in the early 2000's,

1747
01:20:12.920 --> 01:20:16.600
I started seeing more and more patients coming in addicted

1748
01:20:16.600 --> 01:20:20.110
to the opioids that their doctors were prescribing.

1749
01:20:20.110 --> 01:20:23.530
And that was really at the beginning of my own personal

1750
01:20:23.530 --> 01:20:26.390
reeducation around addiction and how to help people

1751
01:20:26.390 --> 01:20:27.280
with addiction.

1752
01:20:27.280 --> 01:20:30.400
And I learned so much from my patients

1753
01:20:30.400 --> 01:20:32.550
and my colleagues about addiction medicine,

1754
01:20:32.550 --> 01:20:35.730
and really it transformed the way that I practice.

1755
01:20:35.730 --> 01:20:38.940
So I assumed that if I just educated my colleagues

1756
01:20:38.940 --> 01:20:41.970
about addiction, they would all also see the light

1757
01:20:41.970 --> 01:20:43.860
and stop prescribing in that way.

1758
01:20:43.860 --> 01:20:48.070
And what I discovered was that, even with re-education,

1759
01:20:48.070 --> 01:20:51.410
even when, for example, I consulted on a patient

1760
01:20:51.410 --> 01:20:53.280
and let them know, oh, by the way,

1761
01:20:53.280 --> 01:20:56.040
this person went to 10 other doctors in the same month

1762
01:20:56.040 --> 01:20:59.230
to get Vicodin, you should stop prescribing for them,

1763
01:20:59.230 --> 01:21:01.270
they didn't stop prescribing.

1764
01:21:01.270 --> 01:21:03.980
And that was really the moment where I thought,

1765
01:21:03.980 --> 01:21:05.930
what is going on here?

1766
01:21:05.930 --> 01:21:07.870
This is really bizarre.

1767
01:21:07.870 --> 01:21:09.890
This is a really good person,

1768
01:21:09.890 --> 01:21:12.410
this is a highly educated person,

1769
01:21:12.410 --> 01:21:16.700
why on earth are they continuing to prescribe in this way?

1770
01:21:16.700 --> 01:21:19.840
And what I then discovered through my research

1771
01:21:19.840 --> 01:21:24.640
is all of the invisible incentives inside of medicine

1772
01:21:24.640 --> 01:21:29.100
that keep doctors prescribing in ways that are orthogonal

1773
01:21:29.100 --> 01:21:30.890
to patients actually getting better.

1774
01:21:30.890 --> 01:21:35.270
And the most shocking discovery for me was the extent

1775
01:21:35.270 --> 01:21:38.320
to which the opioid pharmaceutical industry had essentially

1776
01:21:38.320 --> 01:21:42.730
infiltrated every aspect of medicine to promote opioid

1777
01:21:42.730 --> 01:21:45.400
prescribing and their influence was so enormous

1778
01:21:45.400 --> 01:21:48.470
and so powerful that essentially doctors

1779
01:21:48.470 --> 01:21:52.060
were not able to not prescribe.

1780
01:21:52.060 --> 01:21:56.450
And the vehicles that pressured them into it were, shame,

1781
01:21:56.450 --> 01:21:58.310
basically saying pain is undertreated

1782
01:21:58.310 --> 01:21:59.420
and the reason it's undertreated

1783
01:21:59.420 --> 01:22:02.970
is because you are opioid phobic, afraid of opioids.

1784
01:22:02.970 --> 01:22:04.700
So you need to prescribe more.

1785
01:22:04.700 --> 01:22:06.860
It was things like the joint commission,

1786
01:22:06.860 --> 01:22:09.730
creating guidelines and quality measures that said,

1787
01:22:09.730 --> 01:22:12.880
every doctor has to ask every patient about pain,

1788
01:22:12.880 --> 01:22:14.790
whether or not they look like they're in pain

1789
01:22:14.790 --> 01:22:16.950
and use this pain scale from one to 10

1790
01:22:16.950 --> 01:22:19.520
and prescribe opioids if they endorse pain.

1791
01:22:19.520 --> 01:22:21.910
It was things like the Federation of State Medical Boards

1792
