01:46:26.220 --> 01:46:29.390
That took time, it took an incredible amount

2300
01:46:29.390 --> 01:46:33.433
of tolerating pain and it led to really good things.

2301
01:46:34.990 --> 01:46:38.670
I do not believe that there is a pill that you can take

2302
01:46:38.670 --> 01:46:41.030
or a substance that you can imbibe one time

2303
01:46:41.030 --> 01:46:45.230
or two times or three times that can buy you that,

2304
01:46:45.230 --> 01:46:48.740
it can maybe give you a shadow version of that,

2305
01:46:48.740 --> 01:46:52.780
but in the deeply embedded neurological way,

2306
01:46:52.780 --> 01:46:57.010
that is necessary for sustain wellness and recovery.

2307
01:46:57.010 --> 01:46:59.540
What people want is the spiritual awakening

2308
01:46:59.540 --> 01:47:03.150
without doing the hard work to get there.

2309
01:47:03.150 --> 01:47:05.377
You know, one of my patients once said to me,

2310
01:47:05.377 --> 01:47:06.970
"one of the things that I've learned

2311
01:47:06.970 --> 01:47:09.290
about addiction recovery is,

2312
01:47:09.290 --> 01:47:12.410
the hard way is usually the right way."

2313
01:47:12.410 --> 01:47:17.330
And to me, these psychedelic interventions,

2314
01:47:17.330 --> 01:47:19.760
they're like taking the gondola to the top of the mountain

2315
01:47:19.760 --> 01:47:21.460
instead of walking up.

2316
01:47:21.460 --> 01:47:24.180
Now you could argue that, okay, you know,

2317
01:47:24.180 --> 01:47:26.410
both people got to the top of the mountain,

2318
01:47:26.410 --> 01:47:30.460
but I contend that there's something more enduring

2319
01:47:30.460 --> 01:47:33.880
and better about walking up to that top of the mountain,

2320
01:47:33.880 --> 01:47:35.340
then taking the gondola now.

2321
01:47:35.340 --> 01:47:40.140
And again, I admit I'm probably biased from 20 plus years

2322
01:47:40.140 --> 01:47:45.030
of, you know, treating people in recovery and, you know,

2323
01:47:45.030 --> 01:47:48.110
maybe there are aspects of my personality that I also bring

2324
01:47:48.110 --> 01:47:51.093
to the table that will always kind of favor,

2325
01:47:52.100 --> 01:47:55.330
let's say a more aesthetic approach to life.

2326
01:47:55.330 --> 01:48:00.130
But I find it hard to believe, you know,

2327
01:48:00.130 --> 01:48:03.930
that there's not a cost to pay on the other side.

2328
01:48:03.930 --> 01:48:05.270
Yeah. I appreciate that.

2329
01:48:05.270 --> 01:48:08.180
I mean, I don't know a lot of people who are walking

2330
01:48:08.180 --> 01:48:11.030
the earth enlightened as a result of this,

2331
01:48:11.030 --> 01:48:12.920
if it was really delivering on the promise.

2332
01:48:12.920 --> 01:48:16.350
And I think, you know, it's just interesting to see

2333
01:48:16.350 --> 01:48:18.940
the kind of cultural embrace of this and the vernacular

2334
01:48:18.940 --> 01:48:21.640
that goes into it, whether it's, you know, pot,

2335
01:48:21.640 --> 01:48:23.950
or Ayahuasca the plant medicine,

2336
01:48:23.950 --> 01:48:28.950
or, this is life enhancing as opposed to detrimental,

2337
01:48:30.640 --> 01:48:35.240
like particularly with pot and as a parent of teenagers,

2338
01:48:35.240 --> 01:48:38.180
you know, the way in which it's messaged,

2339
01:48:38.180 --> 01:48:40.640
you would think that this is innocuous

2340
01:48:40.640 --> 01:48:42.550
and that everybody's life would be better

2341
01:48:42.550 --> 01:48:43.839
with a little bit of this.

2342
01:48:43.839 --> 01:48:44.672
Right.

2343
01:48:44.672 --> 01:48:47.200
But for every, you know, Seth Rogen,

2344
01:48:47.200 --> 01:48:51.620
there's a lot of damage that I think is underappreciated

2345
01:48:51.620 --> 01:48:53.830
and not talked enough about.

2346
01:48:53.830 --> 01:48:55.550
Like I know plenty of people in recovery

2347
01:48:55.550 --> 01:48:57.080
whose drug of choice is marijuana.

2348
01:48:57.080 --> 01:49:00.880
And it's far from a innocuous.

2349
01:49:00.880 --> 01:49:04.240
Yeah. And again, I think what I try to communicate

2350
01:49:04.240 --> 01:49:06.950
to people because I think it's under appreciated is,

2351
01:49:06.950 --> 01:49:11.600
you might be fine smoking pot every day for 10 years,

2352
01:49:11.600 --> 01:49:13.850
20 years, maybe even 30 years,

2353
01:49:13.850 --> 01:49:17.540
but eventually it will come and bite you in the butt

2354
01:49:17.540 --> 01:49:20.200
and then it's gonna be really, really ugly.

2355
01:49:20.200 --> 01:49:21.483
So why not stop now?

2356
01:49:22.350 --> 01:49:23.490
Why not stop now?
Right. But that's tough.

2357
01:49:23.490 --> 01:49:25.550
My mind is like, well, I'll deal with that in 30 years.

2358
01:49:25.550 --> 01:49:28.100
If I can do this for 30 years without a problem, like,

2359
01:49:28.100 --> 01:49:29.400
sign me up for that.

2360
01:49:29.400 --> 01:49:32.290
Really, because in 30 years, I mean,

2361
01:49:32.290 --> 01:49:34.378
it's really, really bad do you know?

2362
01:49:34.378 --> 01:49:36.630
I know, so I think it's even in those 30 years,

2363
01:49:36.630 --> 01:49:40.940
it's deceptive because basically you develop tolerance,

2364
01:49:40.940 --> 01:49:44.280
your brain adapts, and then you're using to stave off

2365
01:49:44.280 --> 01:49:45.113
withdrawal from the last dose.

2366
01:49:45.113 --> 01:49:48.100
You're not really feeling better anymore.

2367
01:49:48.100 --> 01:49:50.610
I mean, that's that deceptive piece-

2368
01:49:50.610 --> 01:49:52.370
Where it needed to feel normal.

2369
01:49:52.370 --> 01:49:55.070
Right, and you're not really seeing the impact

2370
01:49:55.070 --> 01:49:56.420
'cause you're in it.

2371
01:49:56.420 --> 01:50:00.040
And I suspect that somebody who's been a chronic daily

2372
01:50:00.040 --> 01:50:03.700
pot smoker for 30 years when they do decide enough's enough

2373
01:50:03.700 --> 01:50:07.610
and they try to stop, that period of acclimating

2374
01:50:07.610 --> 01:50:10.080
your neurochemistry to some level of normal

2375
01:50:10.080 --> 01:50:12.960
is gonna be brutal and very long.

2376
01:50:12.960 --> 01:50:17.750
Brutal, long and in some cases not possible, right?

2377
01:50:17.750 --> 01:50:20.330
I especially have a cohort of older adults

2378
01:50:20.330 --> 01:50:23.715
who've been smoking pot their whole lives, who actually,

2379
01:50:23.715 --> 01:50:28.410
they've lost the brain plasticity to adapt to not using.

2380
01:50:28.410 --> 01:50:31.740
And yet not only is the pot not working for them anymore,

2381
01:50:31.740 --> 01:50:33.260
it's actually turned on them,

2382
01:50:33.260 --> 01:50:35.500
gives them panic attacks, makes them paranoid.

2383
01:50:35.500 --> 01:50:39.470
So here's this thing that they have to keep using every day

2384
01:50:39.470 --> 01:50:40.780
in order to stave off withdrawal,

2385
01:50:40.780 --> 01:50:43.480
but it makes them feel absolutely awful.

2386
01:50:43.480 --> 01:50:45.230
So that's what I mean about the cost.

2387
01:50:45.230 --> 01:50:48.083
Mmh. What about vaping?

2388
01:50:49.130 --> 01:50:50.960
Vaping nicotine, vaping cannabis?

2389
01:50:50.960 --> 01:50:52.700
I guess you can vape anything now, right?

2390
01:50:52.700 --> 01:50:53.930
You can really vape anything, right?

2391
01:50:53.930 --> 01:50:56.220
I mean, I know very little about this other than the fact

2392
01:50:56.220 --> 01:50:57.630
that I'm a parent of teenagers

2393
01:50:57.630 --> 01:50:59.730
and this is like a huge thing.

2394
01:50:59.730 --> 01:51:03.570
The fact that not only are powerful substances

2395
01:51:03.570 --> 01:51:05.340
more readily available,

2396
01:51:05.340 --> 01:51:08.760
they've suddenly become essentially undetectable.

2397
01:51:08.760 --> 01:51:11.250
Right. They're like the little kid chargers-

2398
01:51:11.250 --> 01:51:13.000
In the classroom, they know how to do it

2399
01:51:13.000 --> 01:51:14.800
while they're sitting in class

2400
01:51:14.800 --> 01:51:16.780
and not be detected by the teacher.

2401
01:51:16.780 --> 01:51:19.310
Right. It's odorless.

2402
01:51:19.310 --> 01:51:22.500
It does create a kind of a smoke, but you know,

2403
01:51:22.500 --> 01:51:23.710
it's not the kind of smoke

2404
01:51:23.710 --> 01:51:25.130
that you would get with cigarettes.

2405
01:51:25.130 --> 01:51:26.980
It looks like a little battery charger,

2406
01:51:26.980 --> 01:51:29.120
you can just plug it into your computer.

2407
01:51:29.120 --> 01:51:31.280
So it's essentially undetectable.

2408
01:51:31.280 --> 01:51:35.870
And the scary thing, especially in the nicotine pods

2409
01:51:35.870 --> 01:51:39.020
is that they deliver so much nicotine,

2410
01:51:39.020 --> 01:51:41.680
that kids are ending up with very high blood levels

2411
01:51:41.680 --> 01:51:43.110
of nicotine, much higher than they would

2412
01:51:43.110 --> 01:51:45.060
with normal cigarettes.

2413
01:51:45.060 --> 01:51:48.460
So these kids aren't sleeping, you know, they're jittery.

2414
01:51:48.460 --> 01:51:51.740
Yeah. I mean, the technology has made these things so potent

2415
01:51:51.740 --> 01:51:53.753
and so accessible that it's really scary.

2416
01:51:53.753 --> 01:51:57.261
Are you seeing this show up in your patients

2417
01:51:57.261 --> 01:51:59.650
in your clinic?

2418
01:51:59.650 --> 01:52:00.870
We are seeing a lot of that.

2419
01:52:00.870 --> 01:52:02.690
It's funny in the bay area,

2420
01:52:02.690 --> 01:52:06.120
parents and other advocacy groups mobilized very early

2421
01:52:06.120 --> 01:52:09.180
to raise awareness about vape pens

2422
01:52:09.180 --> 01:52:11.210
and you know, nicotine levels.

2423
01:52:11.210 --> 01:52:16.120
So we've done a lot of community education around this,

2424
01:52:16.120 --> 01:52:19.117
such that we were initially seeing a ton of vaping,

2425
01:52:19.117 --> 01:52:21.980
and I feel like it's dropped off a little bit,

2426
01:52:21.980 --> 01:52:24.720
what we really struggle with frankly,

2427
01:52:24.720 --> 01:52:26.670
most in young people is cannabis.

2428
01:52:26.670 --> 01:52:28.263
MM-hmm, yeah.

2429
01:52:29.930 --> 01:52:34.300
I wanna spend the last section of this

2430
01:52:34.300 --> 01:52:39.300
talking about how to identify somebody

2431
01:52:41.880 --> 01:52:46.030
who is potentially addicted

2432
01:52:46.030 --> 01:52:48.390
or is moving in that direction.

2433
01:52:48.390 --> 01:52:49.970
Like, what are some of the warning signs,

2434
01:52:49.970 --> 01:52:53.370
whether you're a parent or a friend or a brother

2435
01:52:53.370 --> 01:52:56.750
or a colleague given the ubiquitous nature

2436
01:52:56.750 --> 01:52:59.010
with which we're all becoming addicted

2437
01:52:59.010 --> 01:53:01.460
and in this diversity of ways,

2438
01:53:01.460 --> 01:53:03.833
like what are some of the warning signs?

2439
01:53:04.857 --> 01:53:07.680
I think one of the most important warning signs

2440
01:53:07.680 --> 01:53:12.680
is the double life, which is when we're behaving one way

2441
01:53:14.410 --> 01:53:16.410
with the people in our lives,

2442
01:53:16.410 --> 01:53:19.660
and then have this separate life that they're not aware of,

2443
01:53:19.660 --> 01:53:21.290
and that we lie about.

2444
01:53:21.290 --> 01:53:25.020
To me that's a really important early sentinel signal

2445
01:53:26.060 --> 01:53:29.320
that indicates a whole host of different types

2446
01:53:29.320 --> 01:53:34.150
of compulsive over consumption edging toward addiction.

2447
01:53:34.150 --> 01:53:39.150
So, believe it or not, I actually prescribe truth-telling

2448
01:53:40.760 --> 01:53:44.230
to my patients no matter what stage

2449
01:53:44.230 --> 01:53:45.510
of their addiction they are in.

2450
01:53:45.510 --> 01:53:48.150
And even people with little minor addictions, I say,

2451
01:53:48.150 --> 01:53:50.430
try to go this whole month and don't lie

2452
01:53:50.430 --> 01:53:52.370
about what you're using,

2453
01:53:52.370 --> 01:53:54.100
but also don't worry about anything at all.

2454
01:53:54.100 --> 01:53:55.884
Just turns out to be really, really hard for all of us.

2455
01:53:55.884 --> 01:53:56.717
Yeah. And terrifying.

2456
01:53:56.717 --> 01:54:00.113
Yeah. And terrifying, 'cause, we're all, you know, liars,

2457
01:54:01.000 --> 01:54:02.950
it's a sort of part of human nature.

2458
01:54:02.950 --> 01:54:04.882
So I think that's a piece of it.

2459
01:54:04.882 --> 01:54:09.882
You know, often you'll hear people who are in my field

2460
01:54:11.520 --> 01:54:16.520
tell parents that, you should look for changes in function.

2461
01:54:16.520 --> 01:54:19.080
But unfortunately there are a lot of kids

2462
01:54:19.080 --> 01:54:22.280
who can be using a lot of drugs and get straight A's

2463
01:54:22.280 --> 01:54:24.080
and appear to function just fine.

2464
01:54:24.080 --> 01:54:25.410
It may improve their function.

2465
01:54:25.410 --> 01:54:27.210
In many cases, it does.

2466
01:54:27.210 --> 01:54:28.910
Well that's right, right?

2467
01:54:28.910 --> 01:54:30.200
Yeah. It solves a problem.

2468
01:54:30.200 --> 01:54:33.380
Whether that's anxiety or social phobia, you know,

2469
01:54:33.380 --> 01:54:37.230
wanting to be comfortable in groups or manage anger,

2470
01:54:37.230 --> 01:54:42.160
existential, you know, uncomfortableness or whatever it is.

2471
01:54:42.160 --> 01:54:46.710
So I don't really think that function per say,

2472
01:54:46.710 --> 01:54:50.130
is necessarily gonna allow us to detect

2473
01:54:50.130 --> 01:54:52.300
that in our loved ones.

2474
01:54:52.300 --> 01:54:55.263
I really think that this, this kind of,

2475
01:54:56.762 --> 01:54:59.082
obfuscation, I can't even say the word.

2476
01:54:59.082 --> 01:54:59.915
Obfuscation.

2477
01:54:59.915 --> 01:55:01.850
Thank you, obfuscation, you know,

2478
01:55:01.850 --> 01:55:04.000
the sort of smoke and mirrors.

2479
01:55:04.000 --> 01:55:06.410
Even little things like saying, oh, you know,

2480
01:55:06.410 --> 01:55:08.560
I was over there, but then you weren't there,

2481
01:55:08.560 --> 01:55:10.120
you were somewhere else.

2482
01:55:10.120 --> 01:55:12.600
You know, that should be a worry.

2483
01:55:12.600 --> 01:55:13.873
That should be a worry.

2484
01:55:17.185 --> 01:55:20.730
Yeah. I guess that's, I mean, I don't like to say like,

2485
01:55:20.730 --> 01:55:22.983
oh, look for this because the truth is that,

2486
01:55:24.460 --> 01:55:27.410
people are really good at covering up addictive behaviors.

2487
01:55:27.410 --> 01:55:28.550
Yeah. That's what I was thinking.

2488
01:55:28.550 --> 01:55:32.430
Like you can say, well, you know, look for the double life,

2489
01:55:32.430 --> 01:55:36.650
but the addict is very diligent and crafty and protecting

2490
01:55:36.650 --> 01:55:39.620
that second life to be undetectable.

2491
01:55:39.620 --> 01:55:40.530
That's exactly right.

2492
01:55:40.530 --> 01:55:42.030
So when they come into your clinic

2493
01:55:42.030 --> 01:55:44.750
and are at some of being ready to be honest,

2494
01:55:44.750 --> 01:55:47.360
that doesn't mean that they're gonna be ready to be honest

2495
01:55:47.360 --> 01:55:49.060
outside of your office?

2496
01:55:49.060 --> 01:55:50.560
That's for damn sure.

2497
01:55:50.560 --> 01:55:51.393
Yeah.

2498
01:55:51.393 --> 01:55:53.110
Boy, I just had a patient last week

2499
01:55:53.110 --> 01:55:56.520
and we even role played about how she was gonna tell

2500
01:55:56.520 --> 01:55:58.240
her girlfriend that she relapsed.

2501
01:55:58.240 --> 01:56:01.860
And I said, okay, call me tonight, let me know how it went.

2502
01:56:01.860 --> 01:56:03.283
No phone call.
No phone call.

2503
01:56:03.283 --> 01:56:04.281
No phone call.

2504
01:56:04.281 --> 01:56:06.570
But you've been doing this long enough to know

2505
01:56:06.570 --> 01:56:08.603
that on some level, perhaps you weren't expecting

2506
01:56:08.603 --> 01:56:09.436
a phone call.

2507
01:56:09.436 --> 01:56:12.280
Oh, I always hope for it, I always hope for it.

2508
01:56:12.280 --> 01:56:14.720
Because sometimes you know, it works

2509
01:56:14.720 --> 01:56:16.502
and I get the phone call.

2510
01:56:16.502 --> 01:56:19.390
So I always expect it, hope for it,

2511
01:56:19.390 --> 01:56:21.780
but, and yet I'm not surprised when I don't get it.

2512
01:56:21.780 --> 01:56:23.480
And I'll just have to reach out to her again,

2513
01:56:23.480 --> 01:56:26.990
and keep holding her in that space till she's ready,

2514
01:56:26.990 --> 01:56:29.210
keep encouraging her to make that step.

2515
01:56:29.210 --> 01:56:30.500
But yeah, I mean, it's hard.

2516
01:56:30.500 --> 01:56:33.010
And I, you know, I really feel for parents

2517
01:56:33.010 --> 01:56:37.940
because many times when serious addictions come to light

2518
01:56:37.940 --> 01:56:39.540
parents blame themselves and say,

2519
01:56:39.540 --> 01:56:40.950
how could I not have seen that?

2520
01:56:40.950 --> 01:56:42.050
How could I not have known?

2521
01:56:42.050 --> 01:56:45.550
And I just feel like saying like easily,

2522
01:56:45.550 --> 01:56:48.910
you could not have known very easily because, you know,

2523
01:56:48.910 --> 01:56:52.010
people are really good at hiding these behaviors

2524
01:56:52.010 --> 01:56:55.460
and come up with all sorts of really advanced strategies

2525
01:56:55.460 --> 01:56:56.293
for doing it.

2526
01:56:56.293 --> 01:56:57.693
So, you know, don't blame yourself.

2527
01:56:57.693 --> 01:57:01.140
I mean, I don't know what my kids are doing online.

2528
01:57:01.140 --> 01:57:02.320
I mean, you know, now that they're older,

2529
01:57:02.320 --> 01:57:03.490
I did when they were younger.

2530
01:57:03.490 --> 01:57:07.400
Now I have no idea and I'm kind of neurotic about it too.

2531
01:57:07.400 --> 01:57:08.770
But what I try to do is,

2532
01:57:08.770 --> 01:57:10.880
educate them about the pleasure pain balance

2533
01:57:10.880 --> 01:57:13.540
and really demand honesty.

2534
01:57:13.540 --> 01:57:17.590
I say, you know, I can't control what you do,

2535
01:57:17.590 --> 01:57:18.993
but don't lie to me.

2536
01:57:20.210 --> 01:57:22.720
It's a hard balance to strike as a parent.

2537
01:57:22.720 --> 01:57:26.750
You don't wanna be the overly intrusive,

2538
01:57:26.750 --> 01:57:31.070
overly helicoptering parent who's into their business

2539
01:57:31.070 --> 01:57:35.280
too much, but you also can't be checked out either.

2540
01:57:35.280 --> 01:57:39.500
And you know, where that sweet spot is very difficult.

2541
01:57:39.500 --> 01:57:42.090
You know, it's interesting data show that parents

2542
01:57:42.090 --> 01:57:46.480
who are more involved in their kids' lives

2543
01:57:46.480 --> 01:57:49.293
know where their kids are, know their friends,

2544
01:57:50.378 --> 01:57:54.240
check their backpacks, go through their rooms,

2545
01:57:54.240 --> 01:57:57.150
that those kids are actually at decreased risk

2546
01:57:57.150 --> 01:57:58.309
of developing addictions.

2547
01:57:58.309 --> 01:57:59.650
Yes, not surprising.

2548
01:57:59.650 --> 01:58:00.560
Now I agree with you.

2549
01:58:00.560 --> 01:58:04.410
There is a quality to that that if it's intrusive

2550
01:58:04.410 --> 01:58:07.170
and overly controlling can completely backfire.

2551
01:58:07.170 --> 01:58:10.984
Yeah, that will create the opposite reaction

2552
01:58:10.984 --> 01:58:12.350
once they get a little freedom.

2553
01:58:12.350 --> 01:58:13.970
Yes. And Lord knows I've been guilty

2554
01:58:13.970 --> 01:58:15.373
of that in my own parenting.

2555
01:58:15.373 --> 01:58:16.206
(both laughing)

2556
01:58:16.206 --> 01:58:18.030
So your parenting story too?

2557
01:58:18.030 --> 01:58:18.927
No, it's my childhood story, you know?

2558
01:58:18.927 --> 01:58:20.100
Oh interesting.

2559
01:58:20.100 --> 01:58:22.000
Okay, interesting.

2560
01:58:22.000 --> 01:58:23.570
Yeah, okay, so how did that go?

2561
01:58:23.570 --> 01:58:25.430
So I can not make that mistake.

2562
01:58:25.430 --> 01:58:28.250
Well, I, I just, I grew up in a, I mean, you read my book,

2563
01:58:28.250 --> 01:58:30.947
I grew up in a very achievement oriented household

2564
01:58:30.947 --> 01:58:32.437
and there was a lot of attention on me,

2565
01:58:32.437 --> 01:58:36.440
and a lot of expectations and I was very academically

2566
01:58:36.440 --> 01:58:38.010
and athletically motivated,

2567
01:58:38.010 --> 01:58:40.910
but I lived a very structured life.

2568
01:58:40.910 --> 01:58:42.650
Like I never got into trouble,

2569
01:58:42.650 --> 01:58:44.670
I never, you know, broke the rules

2570
01:58:44.670 --> 01:58:45.810
or any of that kind of stuff.

2571
01:58:45.810 --> 01:58:47.520
And when I moved 3000 miles,

2572
01:58:47.520 --> 01:58:50.370
like, no wonder I moved 3000 miles away to college.

2573
01:58:50.370 --> 01:58:53.860
When I was able to put that kind of distance between,

2574
01:58:53.860 --> 01:58:57.850
my upbringing and my current situation, I found myself,

2575
01:58:57.850 --> 01:59:01.430
you know, spinning out of control or just, you know,

2576
01:59:01.430 --> 01:59:05.810
sowing my wild oats in a way that was unhealthy.

2577
01:59:05.810 --> 01:59:09.790
Because I think there wasn't enough freedom that I had,

2578
01:59:09.790 --> 01:59:12.830
and it was, you know, not to overly blame my parents,

2579
01:59:12.830 --> 01:59:14.713
like a lot of it was self-imposed.

2580
01:59:16.200 --> 01:59:17.520
Yeah. So I hear you on that,

2581
01:59:17.520 --> 01:59:19.610
but I'm wondering what could your parents have done

2582
01:59:19.610 --> 01:59:22.735
differently that might have protected you?

2583
01:59:22.735 --> 01:59:23.568
Hmm.

2584
01:59:26.166 --> 01:59:27.330
That's a good question.

2585
01:59:27.330 --> 01:59:32.330
Maybe nothing, or maybe just creating a little bit more

2586
01:59:33.610 --> 01:59:37.340
leeway to kind of get in trouble a couple of times,

2587
01:59:37.340 --> 01:59:38.200
you know?

2588
01:59:38.200 --> 01:59:40.450
'Cause I was so afraid of getting in trouble.

2589
01:59:41.550 --> 01:59:44.520
So that I could have gotten a little bit

2590
01:59:44.520 --> 01:59:45.790
of that out of my system

2591
01:59:45.790 --> 01:59:48.410
or just have that experience younger.

2592
01:59:48.410 --> 01:59:49.243
Yeah.

2593
01:59:51.330 --> 01:59:52.980
Well, I'm gonna think about that.

2594
01:59:54.940 --> 01:59:57.253
So addiction runs in my family and know,

2595
01:59:58.090 --> 02:00:03.090
I am worried about our kids, particularly our sons.

2596
02:00:03.090 --> 02:00:07.060
And, you know, I've tried to be real thoughtful as a parent

2597
02:00:07.060 --> 02:00:12.060
around this to try and give them the best chance,

2598
02:00:14.420 --> 02:00:16.550
knowing that there are no guarantees.

2599
02:00:16.550 --> 02:00:19.840
And I don't know that I've thread the needle.

2600
02:00:19.840 --> 02:00:24.010
I mean, I know times when I have done it wrong

2601
02:00:24.010 --> 02:00:26.370
and being sort of overly intrusive

2602
02:00:27.350 --> 02:00:29.620
and that kind of letting go is something

2603
02:00:29.620 --> 02:00:31.513
that I'm working hard on now.

2604
02:00:32.420 --> 02:00:36.750
I try to give them knowledge of the science,

2605
02:00:36.750 --> 02:00:39.463
knowledge of people's life experiences,

2606
02:00:41.070 --> 02:00:43.370
and also some metacognitive strategies

2607
02:00:43.370 --> 02:00:45.083
along the lines of tell the truth.

2608
02:00:46.610 --> 02:00:51.520
You know, that shame can be both a force for good

2609
02:00:51.520 --> 02:00:53.153
and a force for evil.

2610
02:00:56.270 --> 02:00:59.040
Think about quantity, frequency, potency,

2611
02:00:59.040 --> 02:01:01.220
I mean, all the things I write about in my book.

2612
02:01:01.220 --> 02:01:03.930
But still, I think that what you're saying

2613
02:01:03.930 --> 02:01:08.810
about your childhood is probably true for my kids, you know,

2614
02:01:08.810 --> 02:01:11.350
in an achievement oriented,

2615
02:01:11.350 --> 02:01:13.170
so achievement can be its own addiction,

2616
02:01:13.170 --> 02:01:14.650
and that's the other thing, right?

2617
02:01:14.650 --> 02:01:16.140
Certainly, for me.

2618
02:01:16.140 --> 02:01:19.340
Yeah. Well, and I think for many of us, so,

2619
02:01:19.340 --> 02:01:24.340
and so like, I wonder if our kids, you know,

2620
02:01:24.460 --> 02:01:25.580
if they developed a problem,

2621
02:01:25.580 --> 02:01:28.120
I hope that they would come to me and to us,

2622
02:01:28.120 --> 02:01:30.460
but I do worry that they might not

2623
02:01:30.460 --> 02:01:32.100
because the shame would be so great.

2624
02:01:32.100 --> 02:01:32.980
So anyway.

2625
02:01:32.980 --> 02:01:34.720
Right. And you being an expert in the field-

2626
02:01:34.720 --> 02:01:35.920
Well exactly.

2627
02:01:35.920 --> 02:01:38.770
You know, multiply that possibly.

2628
02:01:38.770 --> 02:01:41.830
I think one of the things I've tried hard to do

2629
02:01:41.830 --> 02:01:44.343
as a parent, and I am curious about you,

2630
02:01:45.310 --> 02:01:47.850
is that I've tried to be very open with my kids

2631
02:01:47.850 --> 02:01:51.430
about my character defects and my mistakes

2632
02:01:51.430 --> 02:01:55.500
so that they know that I'm not perfect.

2633
02:01:55.500 --> 02:01:57.120
Of course they don't think I'm perfect at all.

2634
02:01:57.120 --> 02:02:00.126
In fact, there's almost nothing good about me these days.

2635
02:02:00.126 --> 02:02:02.040
Yeah. But I know what that's like.

2636
02:02:02.040 --> 02:02:05.910
Right. But what I'm trying to say is from very early on,

2637
02:02:05.910 --> 02:02:10.910
I talked a lot about my mistakes and my regrets

2638
02:02:10.950 --> 02:02:14.000
and my shame and the bad things that I've done

2639
02:02:14.000 --> 02:02:15.160
and tried to own up,

2640
02:02:15.160 --> 02:02:17.940
even in our interpersonal relationships,

2641
02:02:17.940 --> 02:02:21.060
in the ways that I'm highly flawed and broken.

2642
02:02:21.060 --> 02:02:24.520
I hope that will be helpful to my kids to give them room

2643
02:02:24.520 --> 02:02:27.233
to feel like, you know, we all make mistakes.

2644
02:02:28.350 --> 02:02:29.720
Yeah. I think that that's true.

2645
02:02:29.720 --> 02:02:30.560
I think that's powerful.

2646
02:02:30.560 --> 02:02:33.080
I mean, that's something that we've definitely practiced

2647
02:02:33.080 --> 02:02:34.560
as parents, you know,

2648
02:02:34.560 --> 02:02:39.470
and I've taken my kids to AA meetings and they know my story

2649
02:02:39.470 --> 02:02:41.930
and we're pretty transparent almost to a fault-

2650
02:02:41.930 --> 02:02:42.763
Yeah, right.

2651
02:02:42.763 --> 02:02:44.120
When it comes to that kind of thing.

2652
02:02:44.120 --> 02:02:47.216
'Cause that's how I'm inculcated in that.

2653
02:02:47.216 --> 02:02:48.330
Like, that's my nature.

2654
02:02:48.330 --> 02:02:51.400
Like I don't carry shame around it anymore.

2655
02:02:51.400 --> 02:02:52.700
I'm fine talking about it.

2656
02:02:54.870 --> 02:02:57.560
But what's interesting, at least with respect

2657
02:02:57.560 --> 02:03:02.560
to our younger kids, we have one who is very extroverted

2658
02:03:03.920 --> 02:03:05.810
and honest to a fault.

2659
02:03:05.810 --> 02:03:07.270
Like the great thing is, is like,

2660
02:03:07.270 --> 02:03:09.157
she'll just tell you everything.

2661
02:03:09.157 --> 02:03:10.130
And sometimes it's shocking.

2662
02:03:10.130 --> 02:03:13.480
Right.
(both laughing)

2663
02:03:13.480 --> 02:03:17.360
And even when she does lie, like she's a terrible liar,

2664
02:03:17.360 --> 02:03:19.670
but she really is honest, and that's great,

2665
02:03:19.670 --> 02:03:22.100
so there's an open channel there.

2666
02:03:22.100 --> 02:03:25.030
The younger one is very internalized

2667
02:03:25.030 --> 02:03:27.030
and so it's harder to know what's going on.

2668
02:03:27.030 --> 02:03:28.260
Yeah.

2669
02:03:28.260 --> 02:03:30.190
And that's just their natural disposition.

2670
02:03:30.190 --> 02:03:31.023
That's right.

2671
02:03:31.023 --> 02:03:32.952
So it's also an individual thing

2672
02:03:32.952 --> 02:03:35.730
with respect to what works for what kid.

2673
02:03:35.730 --> 02:03:36.563
Yes. That's right.

2674
02:03:36.563 --> 02:03:37.570
Which makes it harder, I guess.

2675
02:03:37.570 --> 02:03:39.520
Yes. Just when you think you've figured it out-

2676
02:03:39.520 --> 02:03:41.540
Right. What works for one-

2677
02:03:41.540 --> 02:03:42.939
Doesn't work for the other one.

2678
02:03:42.939 --> 02:03:44.672
That's right.
I know, I know.

2679
02:03:44.672 --> 02:03:46.370
But it sounds like you've done everything

2680
02:03:46.370 --> 02:03:47.520
that you can do.

2681
02:03:47.520 --> 02:03:49.370
You're the psychotic, you're the expert.

2682
02:03:49.370 --> 02:03:50.386
I know, I know.

2683
02:03:50.386 --> 02:03:52.530
That's all we can do-
That's the scary part-

2684
02:03:52.530 --> 02:03:54.410
And if something were to happen,

2685
02:03:54.410 --> 02:03:57.090
you know, the job for you then as you know, is like,

2686
02:03:57.090 --> 02:03:59.140
it's not your fault.

2687
02:03:59.140 --> 02:03:59.973
Well, yeah.

2688
02:03:59.973 --> 02:04:01.270
And I think this is something,

2689
02:04:01.270 --> 02:04:03.270
you know, a lot of parents, like I know my parents,

2690
02:04:03.270 --> 02:04:04.570
like when I ended up in treatment,

2691
02:04:04.570 --> 02:04:07.130
like they blame themselves, they were devastated.

2692
02:04:07.130 --> 02:04:07.963
Yeah.

2693
02:04:07.963 --> 02:04:10.240
And they kept running this, you know,

2694
02:04:10.240 --> 02:04:11.140
narrative in their head.

2695
02:04:11.140 --> 02:04:12.170
Like what did we do?

2696
02:04:12.170 --> 02:04:14.107
And how did we cause this and all of that?

2697
02:04:14.107 --> 02:04:16.910
And a lot of my work with them has been,

2698
02:04:16.910 --> 02:04:19.120
trying to alleviate that in them.

2699
02:04:19.120 --> 02:04:20.740
Right.

2700
02:04:20.740 --> 02:04:24.290
Yeah. I mean, I think it's perfectly possible

2701
02:04:24.290 --> 02:04:26.100
that I'm doing something terribly wrong right now

2702
02:04:26.100 --> 02:04:28.580
in my parenting that I will not realize

2703
02:04:28.580 --> 02:04:29.447
until 20 years later.

2704
02:04:29.447 --> 02:04:30.870
Well, I feel like it's rigged that way.

2705
02:04:30.870 --> 02:04:33.940
Like no matter what you do it turns out

2706
02:04:33.940 --> 02:04:34.960
to be the wrong thing.

2707
02:04:34.960 --> 02:04:35.793
Right.

2708
02:04:37.720 --> 02:04:41.010
I mean, one of the mantras that I've just sort of, I mean,

2709
02:04:41.010 --> 02:04:44.067
I could almost get a tattoo for it is the AA mantra

2710
02:04:44.067 --> 02:04:47.200
"one day at a time," which I think is just such

2711
02:04:47.200 --> 02:04:49.970
a healthy way to be a parent.

2712
02:04:49.970 --> 02:04:54.480
Because, all I can do is just try my best today,

2713
02:04:54.480 --> 02:04:59.480
to be honest and thoughtful, to listen,

2714
02:04:59.970 --> 02:05:04.340
to really listen and to try to be helpful,

2715
02:05:04.340 --> 02:05:06.200
to be a guide.

2716
02:05:06.200 --> 02:05:09.180
Knowing I'm probably gonna make a lot of mistakes,

2717
02:05:09.180 --> 02:05:12.300
but I'm trying, you know, I haven't given up,

2718
02:05:12.300 --> 02:05:13.980
I'm in it, I'm trying.

2719
02:05:13.980 --> 02:05:17.780
And I think the kids can feel that,

2720
02:05:17.780 --> 02:05:21.440
you know, that, like I want to be a good parent.

2721
02:05:21.440 --> 02:05:23.110
I may not always be a good parent,

2722
02:05:23.110 --> 02:05:25.280
but I'm trying to be a good parent.

2723
02:05:25.280 --> 02:05:28.310
And I think that might carry me over, and like,

2724
02:05:28.310 --> 02:05:33.090
you know, help through some of the messed up stuff

2725
02:05:33.090 --> 02:05:35.093
that I am sure that I'm doing.

2726
02:05:36.080 --> 02:05:38.150
But in the event that one of them develops

2727
02:05:38.150 --> 02:05:42.660
some kind of addiction situation, you know,

2728
02:05:42.660 --> 02:05:44.310
sort of having a healthy relationship

2729
02:05:44.310 --> 02:05:49.000
with how you contributed or didn't contribute to that,

2730
02:05:49.000 --> 02:05:52.060
while maintaining an open channel of communication, right?

2731
02:05:52.060 --> 02:05:53.200
Isn't that the key?

2732
02:05:53.200 --> 02:05:54.779
Yes. I think that's right.

2733
02:05:54.779 --> 02:05:57.010
You don't wanna take on too much responsibility

2734
02:05:57.010 --> 02:05:58.880
and this is where like the let it go, let God

2735
02:05:58.880 --> 02:05:59.713
is really useful.

2736
02:05:59.713 --> 02:06:02.150
It's like, you know, I've done what I can do

2737
02:06:02.150 --> 02:06:04.810
and now these are your choices, right?

2738
02:06:04.810 --> 02:06:08.520
And kind of just saying like this is your life like-

2739
02:06:08.520 --> 02:06:09.353
Yeah.

2740
02:06:09.353 --> 02:06:10.287
I think that that's important.

2741
02:06:10.287 --> 02:06:12.340
And the genetic piece is interesting.

2742
02:06:12.340 --> 02:06:15.380
Like, is it true or not like, there's this trope

2743
02:06:15.380 --> 02:06:17.290
that it jumps generations.

2744
02:06:17.290 --> 02:06:18.650
Like my parents weren't alcoholics,

2745
02:06:18.650 --> 02:06:19.810
my grandparents weren't either.

2746
02:06:19.810 --> 02:06:22.550
But so you have it's in your family,

2747
02:06:22.550 --> 02:06:24.440
it's not in your generation,

2748
02:06:24.440 --> 02:06:27.210
but is there this thing where it jumps generations?

2749
02:06:27.210 --> 02:06:31.220
Is that a truism or is that not backed up by science?

2750
02:06:31.220 --> 02:06:34.370
I don't think there's data to support that.

2751
02:06:34.370 --> 02:06:37.070
The data are, you know, looking at family studies

2752
02:06:37.070 --> 02:06:40.500
and twin studies, you know, an identical twin

2753
02:06:40.500 --> 02:06:44.300
with an alcohol use problem will, or an identical twin,

2754
02:06:44.300 --> 02:06:45.700
if their twin has an alcohol problem,

2755
02:06:45.700 --> 02:06:46.950
they're at higher risk.

2756
02:06:46.950 --> 02:06:49.380
If you have a biological parent or grandparents,

2757
02:06:49.380 --> 02:06:52.820
so it can skip the generations, but the biological parent,

2758
02:06:52.820 --> 02:06:55.050
you know, is gonna make a difference too.

2759
02:06:55.050 --> 02:06:57.513
But that's what the studies show.

2760
02:06:58.430 --> 02:07:01.530
But I also just think, you know, again,

2761
02:07:01.530 --> 02:07:03.530
addiction is endemic in the population.

2762
02:07:03.530 --> 02:07:05.550
It's part of how we're wired.

2763
02:07:05.550 --> 02:07:07.630
We are wired to seek out pleasure.

2764
02:07:07.630 --> 02:07:10.130
We wouldn't be here if that weren't true.

2765
02:07:10.130 --> 02:07:13.080
And so, you know, it can pop up anywhere.

2766
02:07:13.080 --> 02:07:15.970
Like you don't have to have it in your family for your kid

2767
02:07:15.970 --> 02:07:18.070
to develop a serious addiction problem.

2768
02:07:18.070 --> 02:07:19.730
And it doesn't mean that you did anything wrong

2769
02:07:19.730 --> 02:07:22.660
or that there was trauma that needs to be uncovered.

2770
02:07:22.660 --> 02:07:23.690
Yeah.
Right?

2771
02:07:23.690 --> 02:07:25.200
It's like wiring.

2772
02:07:25.200 --> 02:07:28.770
Yeah. What about the epigenetic piece?

2773
02:07:28.770 --> 02:07:30.400
This stuff is fascinating, right?

2774
02:07:30.400 --> 02:07:33.870
The idea that your great, great grandparents

2775
02:07:33.870 --> 02:07:36.470
suffered some trauma in childhood

2776
02:07:36.470 --> 02:07:40.650
and the emotional experience of that trauma gets passed down

2777
02:07:40.650 --> 02:07:44.930
genetically and can manifest in some kind of unhealthy

2778
02:07:44.930 --> 02:07:46.990
addiction related behavior.

2779
02:07:46.990 --> 02:07:51.050
Yeah. And I know there are animal studies showing that,

2780
02:07:51.050 --> 02:07:55.810
you know, these non inherited base pairs that get modulated

2781
02:07:55.810 --> 02:07:59.120
through an experience, literally that DNA

2782
02:07:59.120 --> 02:08:01.170
then gets passed to, you know,

2783
02:08:01.170 --> 02:08:03.738
the offspring and their offspring.

2784
02:08:03.738 --> 02:08:08.738
So I guess all of that is plausible in humans as well.

2785
02:08:11.850 --> 02:08:15.410
But, you know, I also wonder

2786
02:08:15.410 --> 02:08:18.460
how much of it is just the culture.

2787
02:08:18.460 --> 02:08:22.660
I mean, we inherit and elaborate on our culture

2788
02:08:22.660 --> 02:08:24.700
as much as we do on our DNA.

2789
02:08:24.700 --> 02:08:27.320
So I think it's gonna be really hard scientifically

2790
02:08:27.320 --> 02:08:31.310
to separate out what is like protein expression

2791
02:08:31.310 --> 02:08:34.800
and what is, oh, this is how we do it in our family.

2792
02:08:34.800 --> 02:08:38.910
You know, we talk about X, but we don't talk about Y or,

2793
02:08:38.910 --> 02:08:42.390
you know, this is how you tolerate hard things

2794
02:08:42.390 --> 02:08:43.513
that come along.

2795
02:08:44.437 --> 02:08:47.270
Yeah. How can you parse those two things?

2796
02:08:47.270 --> 02:08:48.160
It's almost impossible.

2797
02:08:48.160 --> 02:08:50.040
I think almost impossible, yeah.

2798
02:08:50.040 --> 02:08:52.620
Well, finding ourselves mired in a culture

2799
02:08:52.620 --> 02:08:55.040
that is driving us all towards some addiction

2800
02:08:55.040 --> 02:08:57.850
in one form or another, maybe a good way to end this

2801
02:08:57.850 --> 02:09:01.100
is to share, you know, a little bit of wisdom

2802
02:09:01.100 --> 02:09:05.000
or some thoughts for the person who's listening.

2803
02:09:05.000 --> 02:09:07.490
Who's never thought of themselves in any kind of addictive

2804
02:09:07.490 --> 02:09:08.730
context, but is like,

2805
02:09:08.730 --> 02:09:11.240
